// StdToArm64Conversion — arm64 instruction selection: Std → the unified `TargetOp`'s arm64 band.
//
// The counterpart of `StdToX64Conversion.lowerStdToX64`, and it returns the SAME pair — a
// `TargetModule` plus the per-function `ValueClassColumns` — because the class column crosses the
// tier boundary here, at the last pass that can see a Std type (see BackendDispatch.buildArm64Backend
// and TargetDialect's `ValueClassColumn`). The result still uses VIRTUAL registers; the shared
// register allocator colours them into NEUTRAL `RegNum`s and the arm64 encoder maps those to
// `Arm64Register`s one tier below.
//
// ⭐ WHERE arm64 DIVERGES FROM x64, AND WHY IT IS SIMPLER:
//   • 3-ADDRESS ISA — every ALU op takes three INDEPENDENT operands (plain defs, no two-address
//     `Reuse`), so no seed copy is ever emitted; the allocator supplies nothing.
//   • NO FLOAT-EQUALITY SPLIT — arm64 `fcmp` sets Z=0 on an UNORDERED (NaN) result (x64 `ucomisd`
//     sets ZF=1), so `a == b` via `eq` is already FALSE on NaN and `a != b` via `ne` already TRUE.
//     The parity bit and the second `ordered` block x64 needs do not exist here.
//   • A COMPARE MATERIALISES ONLY WHEN ITS BOOLEAN IS READ — the compare/branch fusion census
//     (`CompareFusionCensus`, shared with x64) says whether the branch terminating a compare's own
//     block reads its flags. If it does, the pair is `cmp`/`fcmp` + `b.cond` and no `cset` is emitted;
//     a boolean that is read as a VALUE still gets its `cset`, and a condition that is not a fused
//     compare at all is still truth-tested with `cbnz`. All three shapes can coexist in one block.
//     ⚠ `b.cond` reads NZCV where `cbnz` reads a register, so the fused pair now DOES depend on flag
//     lifetime — see `lowerCondBranch` for the rule and why arm64 needs no rule of its own beyond it.
//   • DIVIDE-BY-ZERO IS NOT A TRAP — AArch64 `sdiv`/`udiv` are DEFINED to return 0 on a zero divisor
//     (no `#DE`, unlike x64's `idiv`), and the spec adopts that: `a / 0` yields 0, and the
//     divide/modulo-by-zero tests are gated to x64-windows (safety.md). So a division lowers to a bare
//     `sdiv` (+ `msub` for the remainder) with NO guard block and NO panic routine — nothing splits the
//     block, so the op walk is a straight one-block-in, one-block-out lowering exactly like x64's.
//
// The OS-primitive lowering (`osExit`/`osAllocPages`/`osWriteStdout`, the M2 runtime floor) bottoms the
// Std-tier slab and String runtimes out in the platform's `exit`/`mmap`/`write` — on macOS through
// libSystem GOT imports (`arm64ImportCall`), on Linux through raw `svc #0` (`arm64Syscall`). Which route
// is asked ONCE, at `arm64OsCallKind` (see Arm64Runtime for why the two OSes differ at all), and the
// three lowerings below share the one `emitOsPrimitiveCall` door rather than each carrying its own
// OS match. These are the ONLY arm64-specific gaps in the heap/String path: the slab allocator,
// refcounting and the whole String runtime are Std-tier and target-neutral, inherited unchanged.

// The AAPCS64 integer ARGUMENT / return registers: argument `k` arrives in (and is passed in)
// `arm64ArgRegisters[k]` = x0..x7, and an integer value returns in x0. The array LENGTH is this file's
// REGISTER capacity (`arm64RegisterArgCapacity`); an argument past it travels in an outgoing STACK slot at
// `[SP + k*8]`, the AAPCS64 layout.
let arm64ArgRegisters = [Arm64Register.x0, Arm64Register.x1, Arm64Register.x2, Arm64Register.x3, Arm64Register.x4, Arm64Register.x5, Arm64Register.x6, Arm64Register.x7]

// The AAPCS64 FLOAT argument registers, in slot order: a FLOAT parameter/argument travels in
// `arm64FpArgRegisters[fpSlot]` = d0..d7, where `fpSlot` is counted by a SEPARATE FP counter from the
// integer `arm64ArgRegisters` (the same independent-file convention x64 uses — see
// `abiFileSlotIndex`). d0 doubles as the float RETURN register (`Arm64FloatReturnReg`), exactly as
// x0 is both the first integer arg register and the integer return register. The whole SIMD file is
// caller-saved for d0..d7 (see `arm64CallerSavedMask`), so a float live across a call is spilled by the
// allocator with no hand-holding.
let arm64FpArgRegisters = [Arm64Register.d0, Arm64Register.d1, Arm64Register.d2, Arm64Register.d3, Arm64Register.d4, Arm64Register.d5, Arm64Register.d6, Arm64Register.d7]

// The primary integer return register (AAPCS64) — where `lowerReturn` moves an integer result, and
// where `lowerCall` reads a callee's integer result from.
export let Arm64IntReturnReg = Arm64Register.x0

// The primary FLOAT return register (AAPCS64) — a float result returns in d0, not x0.
let Arm64FloatReturnReg = Arm64Register.d0

// The dual-register ERROR-FLAG return register (P1.4b) — the arm64 twin of x64's R10. x9 is an AAPCS64
// caller-saved TEMPORARY (x9–x15) and NOT an argument register (x0–x7), mirroring R10's "caller-saved,
// not an argument register, so no return/argument conflict" choice. A `tryCall` reads the callee's flag
// from here; an `errorReturn` writes it here. ⚠ arm64 error tests are per-target gated OUT of this
// machine's suite, so this path is verified by the arm64 BUILD, not by execution.
export let Arm64FlagReturnReg = Arm64Register.x9

// The `mmap` call shape `lowerOsAllocPages` emits: `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
// MAP_ANON|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)`. The POSIX-shared parameters (`MmapKernelChoosesBase`/`MmapProtReadWrite`/
// `MmapAnonPrivateLinux`/`MmapAnonFd`/`MmapNoOffset`) live in `Targets/Shared/PosixMmap.maxon`, one fact
// shared with the x64 isel (OPEN #83).
//
// ⚠ THE FLAG WORD IS THE ONE NUMBER THAT DIFFERS BETWEEN THE TWO OSes this backend serves — everything
// else about the call is POSIX-universal. `MAP_ANON` is 0x1000 on Darwin (`MAP_ANONYMOUS` is 0x20 on
// Linux), over a shared `MAP_PRIVATE` of 0x2 — so the words are 0x1002 and 0x22. The Darwin word stays
// HERE because x64 never targets macOS, so arm64 is the only backend that needs it; the Linux word is
// shared. A blanket value would map a FILE on one OS and hand the slab a mapping that is neither
// anonymous nor zeroed.
let MmapAnonPrivateMacos = 0x1002  // Darwin: MAP_ANON (0x1000) | MAP_PRIVATE (0x2). arm64-macOS only.

// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE THE `_SC_` NUMBER IS WRITTEN, AND WHY THERE IS NO SECOND ONE.**
// `_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN`, the `sysconf` parameter naming "logical processors currently online", read from
// this machine's SDK header (`MacOSX.sdk/usr/include/unistd.h:283`) rather than from memory. ⚠ It is
// DARWIN-NUMBERED: Linux spells the same parameter 84 — and reaches it through a libc FUNCTION rather than
// a syscall — so the two lanes share neither the call nor the number. That is why `OsImportSlot.sysconf`
// carries no Linux route and why `lowerOsCpuCount` asks the route oracle before emitting an import call:
// the Linux answer is not this constant through another door, it is `sched_getaffinity` and a popcount in a
// chunk (`Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxCpuCountChunk`). Those two say what they DO; the number and its
// provenance live here, once.
let DarwinScNprocessorsOnln = 58

// ⭐⭐ **`MSG_NOSIGNAL` — AND THE TWO POSIX LANES DISAGREE ABOUT ITS VALUE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS HERE AND
// NOT IN `PosixRuntime`.** Darwin's is **0x80000** (`MacOSX.sdk/usr/include/sys/socket.h:594`) and Linux's
// is 0x4000 (`LinuxMsgNoSignal`); the flag word is a lowering's argument on both, so each names its own.
//
// ⛔⛔ **WITHOUT IT A SEND ON A CLOSED PEER KILLS THE PROCESS, AND THAT IS MEASURED ON BOTH LANES.** A
// `send` to a socket whose peer has gone raises SIGPIPE, whose default disposition is TERMINATE — and this
// backend installs no signal handlers, so the program dies by signal instead of reporting
// `__ManagedSocketError.sendFailed`. MEASURED with a `socketpair` whose read end was closed: with the flag
// the call answers -1/`EPIPE` and the probe exits 0; without it the probe dies with exit 141 on Darwin and
// on an aarch64 Linux guest alike, and flag 0x4000 on Darwin dies the same way — the value is not shared.
// Windows needs no counterpart: there is no SIGPIPE to suppress, which is why `SendRecvNoFlags` stands
// unqualified on that lane.
let DarwinMsgNoSignal = 0x80000

// The `madvise` advice that DECOMMITS a range on Darwin (F1a). Here rather than in `PosixMmap` for exactly
// `MmapAnonPrivateMacos`'s reason one line up: arm64 is the only backend that targets macOS.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT IS A DIFFERENT ADVICE FROM LINUX'S, NOT A DIFFERENT NUMBER FOR THE SAME ONE, AND IT IS WEAKER.**
// Darwin's `MADV_DONTNEED` does not free anonymous private pages at all; `MADV_FREE` does, but only marks them
// RECLAIMABLE — a write that beats the kernel to them cancels the reclaim, so a page can come back STILL DIRTY.
// Linux's `MADV_DONTNEED` refaults a zero page instead. ⇒ a scavenger may never assume a recommitted page is
// zeroed; `StdDialect.osDecommitPages` carries that obligation in full, and it is the reason these two constants
// have separate names rather than one `MadviseDecommit` hiding the difference.
let MadviseFreeMacos = 0x5  // Darwin: MADV_FREE — reclaimable, and NOT promised zero on the next touch.

// The anonymous-private mapping flag word for `os`. Routed through `arm64OsCallKind` rather than a
// second `match os`, so the set of OSes this backend serves is stated in exactly one place.
export function mmapAnonPrivateFlags(os Os) returns MachineWord
	return match arm64OsCallKind(os) 'route'
		importCall gives MmapAnonPrivateMacos
		syscall gives MmapAnonPrivateLinux
	end 'route'
end 'mmapAnonPrivateFlags'

// The decommit advice for `os` — `mmapAnonPrivateFlags`' twin, routed through the same one door so the set of
// OSes this backend serves is stated in exactly one place. See `MadviseFreeMacos` for why the two differ.
function madviseDecommitAdvice(os Os) returns MachineWord
	return match arm64OsCallKind(os) 'route'
		importCall gives MadviseFreeMacos
		syscall gives MadviseDontNeedLinux
	end 'route'
end 'madviseDecommitAdvice'

// The IP scratch register the isel materialises an immediate — or a folded `base + offset` address —
// into when no immediate op-form fits: a multiplier (arm64 has no multiply-by-immediate), a non-bitmask
// logical immediate (AArch64 `and`/`or`/`xor` #imm encode only rotated bitmasks), an add/sub immediate
// past 24 bits (the imm12 field, optionally shifted by 12, reaches no further), a compare immediate past
// ONE of those fields, or a load/store displacement past the scaled imm12 (`addressFor`). `movz x16, imm`
// (or `add x16, base, #off`) then the op that reads it follows. x16/x17 are the platform IP scratch
// registers — free to clobber for exactly this, and NOT in the allocatable pool, so no VALUE is ever
// coloured into one.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE PRODUCER AND ITS CONSUMER ARE EMITTED ADJACENTLY AND DO NOT STAY ADJACENT, SO "ADJACENT" IS
// NOT THE SAFETY ARGUMENT.** MEASURED: `wide-field-offsets/byte-field-past-the-imm12`'s own golden shows
// a rematerialised constant spliced BETWEEN the fold and the store it addresses through —
// `arm64.add x16, x0, 4096` · `arm64.movImm x1, 0` · `arm64.storeBaseDispReg.byte [x16 + 0], x1`. The
// splitter inserts reloads, spill stores and rematerialised constants wherever a value's use needs one,
// and a use of the CONSUMER is such a site.
//
// What actually makes the scheme safe is that **NOTHING A POST-ISEL PASS SPLICES IN WRITES x16**: a
// spill or reload is a frame-slot form off x29 (`arm64EmitFrameSlot`) — and where the frame outgrows the
// scaled field it folds its address into **x17**, deliberately not x16, for exactly this reason
// (`arm64EmitWideFrameSlot` carries that argument in full); a remat is `arm64MovImm` into an ALLOCATABLE
// register; and SSA destruction's `movRegReg`/`xchgRegReg` go on EDGES — at a block's end or in a split
// block — never between two ops of one lowering. ⇒ **a new post-ISEL splice that can touch x16 breaks
// every pair in this file**, and the fix for such a splice is to give it x17, which no pair spans.
//
// The two ENCODER-TIER x16 clobbers (`arm64FpLoadRdata`, `xchgRegReg`'s GPR swap) are a different
// question and are safe for a different reason: neither is spliced by anything, so neither can land
// between a pair. `arm64FpLoadRdata` is ISEL-emitted (and NOT rematerializable —
// `SplitLiveRanges.opIsConstMaterialization` answers false for it, so the splitter re-emits `arm64MovImm`
// and never it), and `xchgRegReg` is spliced only AFTER coloring, on an edge. Declaring either clobber
// would buy nothing, because x16 is not allocatable — see each op's note in `TargetDialect`.
let Arm64Scratch = Arm64Register.x16

// The published slot offset that means "the scheduler has not started". Zero on every target — the Std tier's
// `__sched_tls_teb_offset` word is slab-zero until `__sched_init_procs` writes it — which is what every reader
// of the current P tests, one tier up. Named because `lowerTlsSlotLoad` compares against it and a bare 0 in
// that compare would read as an ordinary zero rather than as the state it means.
let TlsUninitialisedSlotOffset = 0 as Imm32

// ⭐ The message `osCancelIo` aborts with — see `lowerRuntimeAbort` for why a runtime abort is the
// honest lowering of an op that the Std tier builds unconditionally and this lane can never execute.
//
// ⚠ **THIS CONSTANT USED TO SERVE `osWaitHandles`, WHOSE ABORT MAC8 REPLACED WITH A REAL WAIT.** That arm's
// reason was *"nothing can spawn a child here, so the handle array is always empty"*, and the whole of MAC8
// is the rung that falsified it.
let Arm64NoCancelIoMessage = b"panic: CancelIoEx was reached on arm64-macos, where every pipe read completes in its caller: nothing sets a green thread's `ioParked`, so `__gt_promise_drop`'s IO-cancel arm is unreachable and a build that runs it has a parked reader this lane cannot have created\n"

// The bitmask the LOGICAL `not` flips: a bool lives in the 0/1 domain, so complementing it is
// flipping bit 0 — `eor dest, src, #1` — not the 64-bit `mvn` (which would turn true into -2, still
// truthy). The INTEGER `not` is a separate Std opcode (`bitNot` → `mvn`), so this can never reach one.
let Arm64LogicalNotMask = 1 as Imm32

// The memory-access WIDTH door (`accessWidthFor`) and the byte-offset → displacement door
// (`memoryDisplacement`) are shared with the x64 lowering — see `Targets/Shared/StdLoweringShared.maxon`.
// The width is not ISA-specific; only the encoder that turns `byte`/`word64` into `ldrb`/`ldr` (vs
// `movzx`/`mov`) is. `lowerLoadIndirect` / `lowerStoreIndirect` call them.

// The `[base + disp]` pair a data access finally addresses through, once the selector has decided
// whether the offset has a direct addressing form on this ISA (`StdToArm64Conversion.addressFor`). It
// is either the access's own base and offset, or the IP scratch holding `base + offset` and a `disp` of
// zero — and it is a PAIR rather than two returns because the two only ever mean anything together.
type Arm64MemOperand
	export var base as TargetVReg
	export var disp as Imm32

	static function create(base TargetVReg, disp Imm32) returns Arm64MemOperand
		return Self{base: base, disp: disp}
	end 'create'
end 'Arm64MemOperand'

// A physical arm64 operand, from an `Arm64Register`. The arm64 lowering names its fixed ABI registers
// (x0, d0, x16, …) as `Arm64Register`s; this maps one to its neutral `RegNum` — the number the unified
// vreg carries — in ONE place. The `.rawValue` is bound to a local FIRST, deliberately, exactly as
// `x64PhysReg` does: the bootstrap's union-case argument type-checker cannot lower an
// `EnumMember.rawValue` written directly as a case argument (E9001).
export function arm64PhysReg(reg Arm64Register) returns TargetVReg
	let n = reg.rawValue
	return TargetVReg.physical(n)
end 'arm64PhysReg'

// How many arguments of ONE file AAPCS64 passes in registers — the capacity the shared placement rule
// (`abiArgIsOnStack`) is asked against, read off the array itself so adding a register moves it with no
// second number to update. The arm64 twin of `x64RegisterArgCapacity`, including its precondition: the rule
// asks ONE capacity of whichever file an argument belongs to, so two depths would send floats to stack slots
// the callee still expected in `d` registers.
function arm64RegisterArgCapacity() returns RegisterArgCapacity
	if arm64ArgRegisters.count() != arm64FpArgRegisters.count() 'filesDiffer'
		panic("arm64RegisterArgCapacity: the integer file holds {arm64ArgRegisters.count()} argument registers and the SIMD file {arm64FpArgRegisters.count()} — the shared placement rule asks ONE capacity of both files, so they must be the same depth")
	end 'filesDiffer'
	return arm64ArgRegisters.count() as RegisterArgCapacity
end 'arm64RegisterArgCapacity'

// The largest the OUTGOING STACK-ARGUMENT REGION can ever be on this target: every argument past its
// file's register capacity, at one 8-byte slot each. 448 bytes today.
//
// ⭐ THE ONE HOME OF THAT DERIVATION. THREE arm64 encoder fields are relied on to hold this region — the
// frame record's signed STP/LDP imm7 (504 bytes), `add x29, sp, #imm`'s 12-bit field (4095), and the
// outgoing store's SCALED imm12 (4095 units = 32760 bytes) — and each of the three used to re-derive
// `448` in its own prose. One fact written three times is the bug this project keeps naming, so it is
// COMPUTED here and quoted by each assertion: raising `MaxAbiArgs` moves all three at once instead of
// leaving three comments claiming a number that has stopped being true.
//
// The ceiling is the LANGUAGE's rather than this target's, and it is DIAGNOSED rather than assumed —
// `Parser.requireAbiParamCount` refuses a signature past `MaxAbiArgs` (E2015, pinned by
// `stack-arguments/argument-slots-are-capped-and-the-cap-is-stated`), so no program can present a wider
// region. It is also the WORST case, not a per-call figure: a call splits its arguments between the two
// files and EACH file passes `capacity` of them in registers, so a real region is at most this and
// usually far less.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE THREE FIELDS ARE ORDERED, AND ONLY THE SMALLEST CAN EVER FIRE — SO THE HEADROOM ON
// `MaxAbiArgs` IS SEVEN ARGUMENT SLOTS, NOT FOUR THOUSAND.** The STP/LDP imm7 binds at 504 bytes; the
// other two sit 8x and 65x further out and are shadowed by it, and the prologue that carries the imm7
// check is encoded before any body op, so it is always the panic a reader actually sees. MEASURED at the
// language's own worst case (a 64-argument all-integer call, the widest `requireAbiParamCount` admits):
// the emitted frame record is `stp x29, x30, [sp, #448]` against a 504-byte reach — **56 bytes of
// margin, seven slots**. Raise `MaxAbiArgs` to 72 and arm64 stops compiling.
//
// ⇒ whoever raises it must move the frame RECORD off the imm7 first (the outgoing region cannot simply
// grow past 504 while x29 is placed with an STP offset); the wider fields' assertions are backstops
// behind that one and their advice is not the first thing to do. Recorded here rather than at any of the
// three, because this is the number a reader who is raising the ceiling comes to find.
export function arm64OutgoingArgRegionBytes() returns StackSize
	return ((MaxAbiArgs - arm64RegisterArgCapacity()) as StackSize) * SpillSlotBytes
end 'arm64OutgoingArgRegionBytes'

// The physical register integer argument slot `index` occupies. A slot past the file means the placement
// rule said "register" where the file has none, so it is a compiler-invariant break here, not a user error:
// every caller tests `abiArgIsOnStack` first.
export function arm64ArgRegisterAt(index ParamCount) returns Arm64Register
	return try arm64ArgRegisters.get(index) otherwise panic("arm64ArgRegisterAt: argument index {index} exceeds the {arm64ArgRegisters.count()} register-argument slots — a slot past the file must have been routed to an outgoing stack slot by `abiArgIsOnStack`")
end 'arm64ArgRegisterAt'

// The physical SIMD register FP argument slot `index` occupies — the float twin of
// `arm64ArgRegisterAt`, a compiler-invariant break past the file for the same reason. It must reject
// cleanly here rather than mis-storing from the wrong file.
function arm64FpArgRegisterAt(index ParamCount) returns Arm64Register
	return try arm64FpArgRegisters.get(index) otherwise panic("arm64FpArgRegisterAt: float argument slot {index} exceeds the {arm64FpArgRegisters.count()} SIMD register-argument slots — a slot past the file must have been routed to an outgoing stack slot by `abiArgIsOnStack`")
end 'arm64FpArgRegisterAt'

// The physical register a callIndirect captures its result from — **d0** for a float-returning function
// value, **x0** otherwise (P1.5 #78). The indirect twin of `callResultRegister`, keyed on the `resultType`
// the op carries rather than a callee name (an indirect callee has none). It must agree with the value-class
// column, which classes the result via the SAME `stdTypeIsFloat(resultType)`, so the capture `movRegReg`
// sees two same-file ends (an `fmov` for a float) instead of a cross-file move.
function arm64IndirectResultRegister(resultType StdType) returns Arm64Register
	return Arm64FloatReturnReg if stdTypeIsFloat(resultType) else Arm64IntReturnReg
end 'arm64IndirectResultRegister'

// Map a Std comparison predicate to the arm64 condition code its `b.cond`/`cset` tests, in the DIRECT
// (predicate-holds) sense — every compare here materialises with `cset(cond)` and every branch takes
// its then-target when `cond` holds.
//
// ⭐ THE FAMILY IS CHOSEN BY THE OPERAND TYPE. Integers use the SIGNED family; floats use the
// NaN-SAFE conditions, which is the whole reason the float mapping is not the integer one:
//   • `<` → `mi` (N==1), `<=` → `ls` (C==0 ∨ Z==1): both FALSE on unordered, where `lt`/`le` (which
//     read V) are TRUE. This is IEEE-754's "every relational predicate is false on NaN".
//   • `>` → `gt` (Z==0 ∧ N==V), `>=` → `ge` (N==V): both FALSE on unordered too, because `fcmp` leaves
//     N=0,V=1 there, so N!=V. (v1 uses `hi`/`pl` — but those are for the INVERTED else-branch it emits;
//     in the direct then-branch sense used here, `gt`/`ge` are correct. `Arm64CondCode`'s header
//     carries the same fact at the enum itself, where the wrong edit would be made.)
//   • `==` → `eq`, `!=` → `ne`: correct with NO parity bit, because `fcmp` sets Z=0 on unordered.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND AN UNSIGNED INTEGER TAKES THE THIRD FAMILY — `hs`/`lo`/`hi`/`ls`, which read C where the
// signed codes read N and V.** `cmp` is one subtraction that writes all four flags, so the instruction
// is already both answers and only the CONDITION picks between them; an operand type of `u64` is what
// says which. The x64 twin makes the identical split for the identical reason (`condCodeForPred`), and
// the two must: a `<` that answered differently on the two targets for the same declared ranges is the
// cross-target class this repository gates for.
function arm64CondForPred(pred StdCmpPred, isFloat bool, isUnsigned bool) returns Arm64CondCode
	if isFloat 'float'
		return arm64FloatCondForPred(pred)
	end 'float'
	if isUnsigned 'unsignedInteger'
		return arm64UnsignedCondForPred(pred)
	end 'unsignedInteger'
	return arm64SignedCondForPred(pred)
end 'arm64CondForPred'

// The SIGNED integer condition codes — the reading every Std integer compare had before the ordering
// rule, and still the reading for every operand pair that admits a negative.
function arm64SignedCondForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns Arm64CondCode
	return match pred 'map'
		eq gives Arm64CondCode.eq
		ne gives Arm64CondCode.ne
		lt gives Arm64CondCode.lt
		gt gives Arm64CondCode.gt
		le gives Arm64CondCode.le
		ge gives Arm64CondCode.ge
	end 'map'
end 'arm64SignedCondForPred'

// The UNSIGNED integer condition codes — the exact mirror of `arm64SignedCondForPred`, over the four
// codes that read the CARRY flag rather than N/V. `==`/`!=` are the same `eq`/`ne` in both families,
// because Z is written identically by either reading; only the four ORDERING codes differ, which is the
// whole of what the ordering rule decides.
//
// ⚠ NOT the float family, even though arm64 spells `<=` as `ls` in both. `ls` is C==0 ∨ Z==1 — unsigned
// `<=` — and the float table reaches it for a DIFFERENT reason (it is false on unordered). The two
// tables agree on that one entry and disagree on `lt` (`lo` here, `mi` there), so sharing them would be
// a coincidence doing the work of a rule.
function arm64UnsignedCondForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns Arm64CondCode
	return match pred 'map'
		eq gives Arm64CondCode.eq
		ne gives Arm64CondCode.ne
		lt gives Arm64CondCode.lo
		gt gives Arm64CondCode.hi
		le gives Arm64CondCode.ls
		ge gives Arm64CondCode.hs
	end 'map'
end 'arm64UnsignedCondForPred'

// The NaN-safe FLOAT condition codes (see `arm64CondForPred`). `<`/`<=` take the N-flag / carry
// conditions; `>`/`>=` the signed-shaped ones that `fcmp`'s N=0,V=1-on-NaN makes false on unordered.
function arm64FloatCondForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns Arm64CondCode
	return match pred 'map'
		eq gives Arm64CondCode.eq
		ne gives Arm64CondCode.ne
		lt gives Arm64CondCode.mi
		gt gives Arm64CondCode.gt
		le gives Arm64CondCode.ls
		ge gives Arm64CondCode.ge
	end 'map'
end 'arm64FloatCondForPred'

// The register-FILE doors and the value-class-column producer are TARGET-INDEPENDENT and shared with
// the x64 lowering — see `Targets/Shared/StdLoweringShared.maxon` (`buildValueClassColumns`).
// `lowerStdToArm64` calls it; a value's class is what its Std type says, identically on both targets.

// The three arm64 variable-count shift instructions, and the whole of what the shift lowering needs to
// know about a Std shift opcode: `shl`→`lslv`, `shr`→`asrv` (arithmetic — a signed left operand),
// `shrLogical`→`lsrv` (zero-filling — an unsigned one). Named for the INSTRUCTIONS, like x64's twin.
enum Arm64ShiftInstr
	lsl
	asr
	lsr
end 'Arm64ShiftInstr'

function arm64ShiftInstrOf(opcode StdBinOpcode) returns Arm64ShiftInstr
	return match opcode 'o'
		shl gives Arm64ShiftInstr.lsl
		shr gives Arm64ShiftInstr.asr
		shrLogical gives Arm64ShiftInstr.lsr
		add to bitXor panic("arm64ShiftInstrOf: {opcode.rawValue} is not a shift — only `shl`/`shr`/`shrLogical` name a variable-shift instruction")
		div to max panic("arm64ShiftInstrOf: `{opcode.name}` is a FLOAT-ONLY opcode, not a shift")
		mulHighSigned panic("arm64ShiftInstrOf: `mulHighSigned` is a multiply, not a shift")
	end 'o'
end 'arm64ShiftInstrOf'

// The arm64 divide instruction a `IntegerSignedness` names — `sdiv` or `udiv`, which differ in one
// encoding bit and in nothing else (same 3-address shape, same non-trapping zero-divisor behaviour).
// Beside `arm64ShiftInstrOf` because it is the same kind of mapping: a front-end signedness fact
// turned into the one instruction that implements it, in the one place the answer is spelled.
function arm64DivideOp(dest TargetVReg, lhs TargetVReg, rhs TargetVReg, signedness IntegerSignedness) returns TargetOp
	return match signedness 's'
		signed gives TargetOp.arm64Sdiv(dest, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs)
		unsigned gives TargetOp.arm64Udiv(dest, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs)
	end 's'
end 'arm64DivideOp'

// Assert a float lowering entry really has an f64 (the whole SSE/NEON scalar path is f64: `fadd`,
// `fcmp`, an 8-byte `.rdata` island). An f32 is 4 bytes and a real gap; nothing produces one.
function arm64RequireScalarDouble(t StdType, what String)
	if t != StdType.f64 'notDouble'
		panic("{what}: {t.name} reached the arm64 FP lowering, which is scalar-DOUBLE throughout (an 8-byte `.rdata` island loaded by `ldr d`) — an f32 needs the single-precision path. Maxon's `float` is f64, so nothing produces one.")
	end 'notDouble'
end 'arm64RequireScalarDouble'

export function lowerStdToArm64(stdModule StdModule, target Target, globalData GlobalDataTable, funcNames FunctionNameIndex, usesGt bool) returns (TargetModule, ValueClassColumns)
	var lowering = StdToArm64Lowering.create(stdModule, target: target, globalData: globalData, funcNames: funcNames, usesGt: usesGt)
	// x64's twin, and for its stated reason: this is where the Std module stops being appended to, and its
	// column is read by index below.
	let stdOpCount = stdModule.ops.count()
	stdModule.spans.requireParallelTo(stdOpCount, what: "at the Std tier's end")

	// The Target module carries spans exactly when the Std module it lowers from does. Armed BEFORE the
	// skeleton, because `prepareSkeleton` is the first thing that can append.
	lowering.targetModule.spans.armed = stdModule.spans.armed
	lowering.prepareSkeleton()
	// BY INDEX, never by iteration: `lowerFunction` MUTATES the function it lowers (the divide-by-zero
	// guard appends blocks to its `blockRefs` and bumps `nextBlockId`), so no `srcFunc` borrowed out of
	// `stdModule.functions` may be handed to it — the rule `splitCriticalEdges` states for the same
	// reason, enforced by the borrow checker (E3019).
	for funcIdx in 0 upto stdModule.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		lowering.lowerFunction(funcIdx)
	end 'eachFunc'

	// The value-class column producer is the shared, target-independent `buildValueClassColumns` (see
	// `Targets/Shared/StdLoweringShared.maxon`); bind it FIRST so its borrow of `targetModule` closes
	// before the tuple moves `targetModule` out.
	let valueClasses = buildValueClassColumns(lowering.stdModule, targetModule: lowering.targetModule, funcNames: lowering.funcNames)
	return (lowering.targetModule, valueClasses)
end 'lowerStdToArm64'

type StdToArm64Lowering
	// Exported like `targetModule`: `lowerStdToArm64` hands both to the shared `buildValueClassColumns`.
	export var stdModule as StdModule
	// The compilation target — read ONLY by the three OS-primitive lowerings, which differ between
	// arm64-macOS and arm64-linux (import call vs. raw syscall, and the mmap flag word). Every other
	// lowering in this file is OS-invariant: the ISA is the same and shv2 emits no varargs and no
	// sub-64-bit values, so Apple's caller-extends-small-ints divergence is satisfied vacuously.
	var target as Target
	// The backend's data table — where a float constant's `.rdata` island is registered, resolved by
	// the arm64 concat once sections are laid out.
	var globalData as GlobalDataTable
	export var targetModule as TargetModule
	// The module's CHECKED name index (`FunctionNameIndex`, A2l), built by `eliminateDeadFunctions` over the
	// array it left behind and threaded down from `buildBackend`. It answers "what register FILE does this
	// callee's result come back in?" — d0 for a float-returning callee, x0 otherwise (see
	// `callResultRegister`) — WITHOUT a second name-keyed map: `calleeResultClass` reads the callee's own
	// `returnType` through it, so a float result's CAPTURE register and its allocation CLASS are the one
	// fact. Exported like `targetModule`: `lowerStdToArm64` threads the same index into the column.
	export let funcNames as FunctionNameIndex
	// Callee name → its parameters' register FILES as a bitmask. Built ONCE here, FROM the index above — so
	// its key set is the checked one — and read by `emitCallArgMoves` to route each argument to a GPR
	// (x0..x7) or a SIMD (d0..d7) arg register by the callee's declared parameter type. It is a real FOLD
	// over a parameter list rather than a field read, which is why it stays a map where the result-class map
	// did not. Only the argument routing consults it (the value-class column classes a `param` result from
	// its own carried f64 type), so it is not threaded into `buildValueClassColumns`.
	var calleeParamClasses as CalleeParamClassMap

	// EC16 — the `base + index*scale` chains this function's element accesses spell (`ScaledIndexFolds`,
	// in the shared lowering). arm64 answers `accessWidthScaleAtZeroOffset`: `LDR Xt, [Xn, Xm, LSL #3]`
	// carries the whole address, but its `S` bit selects a shift of 0 or exactly log2(the access size)
	// and the register-offset encoding has no displacement field — so the memory fold lands here for the
	// `word64` element at stride 8 that every array of a declared type spells, and nowhere else. The
	// narrowing is the ENCODING's; `StdLoweringShared.indexedFormCarries` states it once.
	var scaledIndexFolds as ScaledIndexFolds

	// The scratch `MemoryIndexScaleMatch` `arm64MulImmForm` writes its `addShifted` answer through — a
	// field rather than a local for `ScaledIndexFolds.operands`' measured reason: a fresh heap object per
	// constant multiply of every function the backend lowers.
	var mulScale as MemoryIndexScaleMatch

	// Which compares this function keeps in NZCV and which must write their boolean to a register — the
	// TARGET-INDEPENDENT analysis both native isels read (`CompareFusionCensus`, in the shared lowering).
	// Created ONCE and recomputed per function, like `scaledIndexFolds` above.
	var compareFusion as CompareFusionCensus

	// Whether the green-thread scheduler was installed, and so whether a `blocking` op is wrapped in the
	// syscall bracket (`lowerBodyOp`). A program with no scheduler has no machine to announce itself to and
	// no processor to lose, so its lowering is byte-identical to what it would be without this door.
	let usesGt as bool

	static function create(stdModule StdModule, target Target, globalData GlobalDataTable, funcNames FunctionNameIndex, usesGt bool) returns StdToArm64Lowering
		return Self{stdModule: stdModule, target: target, globalData: globalData, targetModule: TargetModule.create(), scaledIndexFolds: ScaledIndexFolds.create(), mulScale: MemoryIndexScaleMatch.create(), compareFusion: CompareFusionCensus.create(), funcNames: funcNames, calleeParamClasses: buildCalleeParamClasses(funcNames), usesGt: usesGt}
	end 'create'

	// Whole-module setup: relocate every StdModule block 1:1 into `targetModule.blocks` (preserving the
	// flat-block indices `IrFunction.blockRefs` references) and transfer every function. Both
	// `blockArgs` (the phis) and `branchEdges` (per-predecessor incoming values) carry across so the phi
	// model survives into the Target tier intact (SSA destruction runs after coloring). Mirrors x64.
	function prepareSkeleton()
		for srcBlock in self.stdModule.blocks 'relocateBlocks'
			var dstBlock = IrBlock.create(srcBlock.id, label: srcBlock.label)
			dstBlock.blockArgs = srcBlock.blockArgs
			for srcEdge in srcBlock.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
				dstBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(srcEdge.targetBlockId, argIds: srcEdge.argIds))
			end 'eachEdge'
			self.targetModule.blocks.push(dstBlock)
		end 'relocateBlocks'

		for srcFunc in self.stdModule.functions 'transferFuncs'
			self.targetModule.functions.push(srcFunc)
		end 'transferFuncs'
	end 'prepareSkeleton'

	// ==========================================================================
	// Per-function lowering
	// ==========================================================================

	function lowerFunction(funcIdx FuncIndex)
		// Every read of the source function happens HERE, before the walk, so the walk holds no borrow of it
		// while `applyOutgoingArgSize` writes to the TARGET function of the same index (E3019).
		self.prepareFunctionTables(funcIdx)
		let outgoingArgBytes = self.lowerFunctionBlocks(funcIdx)
		self.applyOutgoingArgSize(funcIdx, bytes: outgoingArgBytes)
	end 'lowerFunction'

	// The per-function analyses, all READS, decided before the walk in a SCOPED read of the source
	// function: the borrow ends with this call, which is `lowerFunction`'s stated reason for reading
	// everything up front. Both must precede the walk because a compare is lowered long before the branch
	// or the phi edge that decides what it has to produce.
	function prepareFunctionTables(funcIdx FuncIndex)
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		self.scaledIndexFolds.computeForFunction(self.stdModule, func: srcFunc, memoryForm: IndexedMemoryForm.accessWidthScaleAtZeroOffset)
		self.compareFusion.computeForFunction(self.stdModule, func: srcFunc)
	end 'prepareFunctionTables'

	// Walk the function's blocks, and return the OUTGOING stack-argument bytes its largest call needs — a
	// running MAX, not a sum, because the region is REUSED by every call site. Only shv2-to-shv2 calls
	// contribute on arm64: its OS primitives (`write`, `mmap`, `exit`) are all ≤6-argument, register-only,
	// which is why there is no `stdOpOutgoingArgBytes` table here to mirror x64's.
	function lowerFunctionBlocks(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns StackSize
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		let returnType = srcFunc.returnType
		// This function's OWN parameter files, folded ONCE — every `param` op then reads a scalar instead of
		// re-walking the parameter types, which is what the per-parameter `computeParamSlotIndex` did.
		let paramFloatMask = floatParamMaskOf(srcFunc.paramTypes)
		var outgoingArgBytes = 0 as StackSize
		// A 1:1 lowering: every op of a Std block lowers into the SAME Target block, and no pass in this
		// file mints new blocks during the walk (the divide-by-zero split that once did is gone — `a / 0`
		// is a bare `sdiv` now). So `blockRefs` is fixed across the walk and iterating it directly is safe.
		for blockRef in srcFunc.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let blockOutgoing = self.lowerOneBlock(blockRef, returnType: returnType, paramFloatMask: paramFloatMask)
			if blockOutgoing > outgoingArgBytes 'largerArgRegion'
				outgoingArgBytes = blockOutgoing
			end 'largerArgRegion'
		end 'eachBlock'

		// The cursor belongs to THIS function's walk; the prologue pass and the runtime band append after
		// it and are nobody's statement.
		self.targetModule.spans.current = NoSpanId

		return outgoingArgBytes
	end 'lowerFunctionBlocks'

	// Record the function's outgoing stack-argument reservation on the TARGET function — the arm64 twin of
	// x64's `applyOutgoingArgSize`, in a scope holding no `srcFunc` borrow of the same object. Skips the
	// assignment when zero, so a function whose calls all fit the register files is untouched.
	function applyOutgoingArgSize(funcIdx FuncIndex, bytes StackSize)
		if bytes == 0 'none'
			return
		end 'none'
		var func = self.targetModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		func.outgoingArgSize = bytes
	end 'applyOutgoingArgSize'

	// Lower ONE Std block's ops and terminator, both into `blockRef`'s Target block, and return the largest
	// outgoing stack-argument region any of its calls needs. The terminator's phi edges were copied onto
	// that block by `prepareSkeleton` and stay there — nothing splits the block, so there is no
	// continuation to move them to.
	function lowerOneBlock(blockRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType, paramFloatMask ArgFloatMask) returns StackSize
		let opCount = self.stdBlockOpCount(blockRef)
		var outgoingArgBytes = 0 as StackSize
		for i in 0 upto opCount 'eachOp'
			let stdOp = self.stdBlockOpAt(blockRef, index: i)
			let opOutgoing = self.stdOpOutgoingArgBytes(stdOp)
			if opOutgoing > outgoingArgBytes 'largerArgRegion'
				outgoingArgBytes = opOutgoing
			end 'largerArgRegion'
			// The Target ops this one call appends inherit the Std op's span — x64's twin, same reason.
			self.targetModule.spans.current = self.stdModule.spans.at(self.stdBlockOpIndexAt(blockRef, index: i))
			self.lowerBodyOp(stdOp, dstRef: blockRef, returnType: returnType, paramFloatMask: paramFloatMask)
		end 'eachOp'

		if self.stdBlockHasTerminator(blockRef) 'hasTerm'
			let termOp = self.stdBlockTerminator(blockRef)
			self.targetModule.spans.current = self.stdModule.spans.at(self.stdBlockTerminatorIndex(blockRef))
			self.lowerTerminatorOp(termOp, dstRef: blockRef, returnType: returnType)
		end 'hasTerm'
		return outgoingArgBytes
	end 'lowerOneBlock'

	// The OUTGOING stack-argument bytes an `StdOp`'s arm64 lowering needs: the arguments a call passes past
	// the eight register slots of their file, and zero for everything else. The arm64 counterpart of x64's
	// same-named method — SHORTER, because AAPCS64 has no shadow space and arm64's OS primitives are all
	// register-only, so the only contributors are the four call ops. Deliberately NOT exhaustive over
	// `StdOp` by hand: the four call variants are named and every other op gives 0 through one range arm
	// pair, which cannot silently swallow a band-appended op the way an over-wide range would, because a new
	// op appended at the END of its band lands past `osReadWallClock` and fails to compile here.
	function stdOpOutgoingArgBytes(op StdOp) returns StackSize
		return match op 'o'
			const to param gives 0
			call(_, callee, args) gives self.callOutgoingArgBytes(calleeArgFloatMask(self.calleeParamClasses, callee: callee, argCount: args.count(), registerCapacity: arm64RegisterArgCapacity()), argCount: args.count())
			retVoid to rdataAddr gives 0
			tryCall(_, _, callee, args) gives self.callOutgoingArgBytes(calleeArgFloatMask(self.calleeParamClasses, callee: callee, argCount: args.count(), registerCapacity: arm64RegisterArgCapacity()), argCount: args.count())
			errorReturn to funcAddr gives 0
			callIndirect(_, _, _, argFloatMask, args) gives self.callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask, argCount: args.count())
			osPanic to switch gives 0
			witnessCall(_, _, _, _, argFloatMask, args) gives self.callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask, argCount: args.count())
			witnessTryCall(_, _, _, _, _, argFloatMask, args) gives self.callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask, argCount: args.count())
			osPipeCreate to osReadWallClock gives 0
			// ⭐ MAC4/5: the file and directory bands DO lower here now, and they still reserve nothing — every
			// one passes at most FOUR arguments (`ReadFile`'s handle/buffer/length/out-param is the widest),
			// inside AAPCS64's x0..x7, so nothing overflows onto the outgoing-argument stack.
			osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes gives 0
			// A rendered panic calls `mrt_panic` with one REGISTER argument, exactly as `osPanic` does.
			osPanicValue gives 0
			// x64-windows only, like the file-IO band above and for the same ruling.
			osCommandLine gives 0
			// ⭐ MAC6: the standard handle lowers here and CALLS NOTHING — a selector becomes a descriptor by
			// arithmetic — so it reserves nothing for a stronger reason than a refused op does.
			osStdHandle gives 0
			// MAC2: its W5 band-mate DOES lower here, and still reserves nothing — `lowerOsExecutablePath`
			// hands the chunk two arguments, inside AAPCS64's x0..x7, so nothing overflows onto the
			// outgoing-argument stack.
			osExecutablePath gives 0
			// W35: an all-register `write` — no stack argument on either stream.
			osWriteStderr gives 0
			// A frame address computes an address; it calls nothing and so passes no outgoing argument.
			stackRecordAddr gives 0
			// The DebugStream band lowers here through chunks, and still reserves nothing: each hands its chunk at
			// most two arguments, inside AAPCS64's x0..x7. The variadic `shm_open` DOES need a stack argument,
			// and the region for it belongs to the chunk's own frame (`Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction`), not to
			// the frame of whatever called it.
			osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs gives 0
			// The rest of the appended band, and it now answers 0 for TWO different reasons rather than one.
			// The four attached-subprocess host primitives and the `ws2_32` socket band are x64-windows only and
			// never reach an arm64 frame, so they reserve nothing exactly as every other refused op does. The
			// machine query and the thread-CPU read DO lower here (`lowerOsCpuCount`, `lowerOsThreadCpuTicks`) and
			// still reserve nothing, for `osDecommitPages`' reason one arm down: each passes ONE argument, inside
			// AAPCS64's x0..x7, so nothing overflows onto the outgoing-argument stack.
			// ⛔ This said the whole range "is x64-windows only and never reaches an arm64 frame", which stopped
			// being true when those two landed. The ANSWER was right throughout, which is what made the wrong
			// reason survive — and a reason is what the next person adding a STACK-ARGUMENT OS call to this range
			// would read before deciding they owe no reservation.
			osHandleSetInherit to osThreadCpuTicks gives 0
			// F1a: unlike the band above, this one DOES lower here — and it still reserves nothing, because
			// `madvise` takes three register arguments, inside AAPCS64's x0..x7. Nothing overflows onto the
			// outgoing-argument stack.
			osDecommitPages gives 0
			// F1b: the byte fill calls nothing at all — it is a store ladder emitted inline — so there is no
			// outgoing-argument region for it to reserve.
			memFill gives 0
			// F1c: both lower here too, and both are `mmap` — six arguments, which AAPCS64 passes in x0..x5 and
			// Linux's `svc` reads from the same six. Nothing reaches the stack.
			osCommitPages to osReservePages gives 0
			// F2: the two atomics are inline instruction sequences and reserve nothing; the thread token is a
			// system-register read on macOS and a ZERO-argument syscall on Linux, and a syscall never touches the
			// stack. So none of the three needs an outgoing-argument region.
			atomicCas to currentThreadToken gives 0
			// G1: the three TLS ops and the event signal are x64-windows only and are REFUSED below rather than
			// lowered, so no frame is reserved for arguments that will never be marshalled here.
			tlsAlloc to osSetEvent gives 0
			// MAC2: the priority door lowers here, and reserves nothing — `setpriority` takes three register
			// arguments and `getpriority` two, all inside AAPCS64's x0..x7.
			osEnterBackgroundPriority gives 0
			// MAC4: two register arguments through the shared `creat`, exactly as `osFileOpenWrite` above.
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable gives 0
			osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree gives 0
			// G3: an ordered access marshals nothing — one `add` into the IP scratch and one instruction.
			loadAcquire or storeRelease gives 0
			// The terminal question lowers here on the macOS route and still reserves nothing: it hands its
			// chunk ONE argument, inside AAPCS64's x0..x7, so nothing overflows onto the outgoing-argument
			// stack. The Linux route is refused below and marshals nothing at all.
			osHandleFileType gives 0
			// The named-section consumer pair reserves nothing for the producer band's reason one arm up: two
			// arguments each, and the variadic call's stack word is the chunk's own.
			osCreateNamedSegment to osUnpublishNamedSegment gives 0
		end 'o'
	end 'stdOpOutgoingArgBytes'

	// The frame bytes ONE call needs for the arguments that overflow the register files — `abiStackSlotsBefore`
	// over the whole argument list is how many landed on the stack, one `SpillSlotBytes` slot each. AAPCS64
	// puts them at `[SP + 0 ..)`, with no shadow space, so the region IS the slot count times the stride.
	function callOutgoingArgBytes(argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, argCount ParamCount) returns StackSize
		return (abiStackSlotsBefore(argFloatMask, index: argCount, capacity: arm64RegisterArgCapacity()) as StackSize) * SpillSlotBytes
	end 'callOutgoingArgBytes'

	// Scoped reads of a Std block: each returns a scalar or a COPIED op, never a borrowed block, so the
	// walk never holds a `stdModule.blocks` borrow across an `emit` into `targetModule` (E3019).
	function stdBlockOpCount(blockRef BlockRef) returns BlockOpPos
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdBlockOpCount: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		return block.opRefs.count()
	end 'stdBlockOpCount'

	// The flat `stdModule.ops` index a block names at `index` — the coordinate a span is keyed by, which
	// `stdBlockOpAt` resolves away. Read through a function for that helper's borrow reason.
	function stdBlockOpIndexAt(blockRef BlockRef, index BlockOpPos) returns OpIndex
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdBlockOpIndexAt: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		return try block.opRefs.get(index) otherwise panic("stdBlockOpIndexAt: opRefs.get OOB at {index}")
	end 'stdBlockOpIndexAt'

	// The flat `stdModule.ops` index of a block's terminator, for the same reason.
	function stdBlockTerminatorIndex(blockRef BlockRef) returns OpIndex
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdBlockTerminatorIndex: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		return block.terminatorIndex()
	end 'stdBlockTerminatorIndex'

	function stdBlockOpAt(blockRef BlockRef, index BlockOpPos) returns StdOp
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdBlockOpAt: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		let opIdx = try block.opRefs.get(index) otherwise panic("stdBlockOpAt: opRefs.get OOB at {index}")
		return try self.stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("stdBlockOpAt: opIdx {opIdx} not in stdModule.ops")
	end 'stdBlockOpAt'

	function stdBlockHasTerminator(blockRef BlockRef) returns bool
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdBlockHasTerminator: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		return block.hasTerminatorOp()
	end 'stdBlockHasTerminator'

	function stdBlockTerminator(blockRef BlockRef) returns StdOp
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdBlockTerminator: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		return try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("stdBlockTerminator: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
	end 'stdBlockTerminator'

	// Append `op` to the Target block named by `dstRef`, in a scope that holds the block borrow only for
	// the append.
	function emit(dstRef BlockRef, op TargetOp)
		var block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(dstRef) otherwise panic("emit: dstRef {dstRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		self.targetModule.appendOp(block, op: op)
	end 'emit'

	function emitCondBranch(dstRef BlockRef, op TargetOp)
		var block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(dstRef) otherwise panic("emitCondBranch: dstRef {dstRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		self.targetModule.appendCondBranch(block, op: op)
	end 'emitCondBranch'

	function emitTerminator(dstRef BlockRef, op TargetOp)
		var block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(dstRef) otherwise panic("emitTerminator: dstRef {dstRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(block, op: op)
	end 'emitTerminator'

	// Lower a regular (non-terminator) Std op into the block named by `dstRef`. Every op lowers into the
	// SAME block — nothing splits the walk (div/mod is a bare `sdiv`/`msub`, not a guarded block).
	//
	// The syscall bracket, at the one dispatch door that covers every road a blocking op takes here — a
	// hand-assembled chunk reached by `bl` (`lowerHostChunkValue`), an isel-inline libSystem import
	// (`emitArm64GtGatedImportCall`) and a raw `svc` alike; `__con_read_stdin`, the read this exists for,
	// takes the first. WHICH ops take it is `opTakesSyscallBracket`, in the shared lowering; only the `bl`
	// that spells the two announcements is this ISA's.
	function lowerBodyOp(op StdOp, dstRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType, paramFloatMask ArgFloatMask)
		let bracketed = opTakesSyscallBracket(op, target: self.target, usesGt: self.usesGt)

		if bracketed 'announceTheCall'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(SchedEnterSyscallName))
		end 'announceTheCall'

		match op 'lower'
			const(resultId, value, valueType) then self.lowerConst(resultId, value: value, valueType: valueType, dstRef: dstRef)
			binOp(resultId, lhs, rhs, opcode, operandType) then self.lowerBinOp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstRef: dstRef)
			unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, operandType) then self.lowerUnaryOp(resultId, operand: operand, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstRef: dstRef)
			cmp(resultId, lhs, rhs, pred, operandType) then self.lowerCmp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstRef: dstRef)
			// The immediate forms' `operandType` (X5) is a WIDTH, and every integer instruction this
			// backend selects is the 64-bit `X`-register form whatever the Std type says (`Arm64OpAddImm`
			// 0x91…, `Arm64OpSubImm` 0xD1…, `Arm64OpSubsImm` (the `cmp` alias) 0xF1…, `Arm64OpMul` 0x9B…, `Arm64OpSdiv`
			// 0x9AC…, every one with `sf` set) — so `binOpImm` drops it for the reason the x64 arm states,
			// not overlooked.
			//
			// ⚠ `cmpImm` THREADS it, exactly as the x64 arm does and for the same reason: `lowerCmpImm`
			// has to answer "is this a float compare?" for the condition code, and it answered with a
			// hardcoded `isFloat: false` — the third spelling of a fact `foldConstOperands`'s float guard
			// already owns and the op now carries.
			binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode, _) then self.lowerBinOpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: imm, opcode: opcode, dstRef: dstRef)
			cmpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, pred, operandType) then self.lowerCmpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: imm, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstRef: dstRef)
			div(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then self.lowerDivMod(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, isRemainder: false, signedness: signedness, dstRef: dstRef)
			mod(resultId, lhs, rhs, signedness) then self.lowerDivMod(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, isRemainder: true, signedness: signedness, dstRef: dstRef)
			param(resultId, index, paramType) then self.lowerParam(resultId, index: index, paramType: paramType, paramFloatMask: paramFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
			call(resultId, callee, args) then self.lowerCall(resultId, callee: callee, args: args, dstRef: dstRef)
			ret(retValId) then self.lowerReturn(retValId, dstRef: dstRef, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			retVoid then self.lowerVoidReturn(dstRef, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			condBranch then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `condBranch` appeared as a body op (lowering bug — a branch is always a block terminator)")
			branch then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `branch` appeared as a body op (lowering bug — a branch is always a block terminator)")
			globalAddr(resultId, label) then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LeaGlobal(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), label: label))
			loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) then self.lowerLoadIndirect(resultId, addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueType: valueType, dstRef: dstRef)
			storeIndirect(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) then self.lowerStoreIndirect(addrId, offset: offset, valueId: valueId, valueType: valueType, dstRef: dstRef)
			// G3: `LDAR`/`STLR` — the ordered pair, whose address the fold above has to materialise because
			// neither instruction has a displacement field. This is the lane the ops exist for.
			loadAcquire(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) then self.lowerLoadAcquire(resultId, addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueType: valueType, dstRef: dstRef)
			storeRelease(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) then self.lowerStoreRelease(addrId, offset: offset, valueId: valueId, valueType: valueType, dstRef: dstRef)
			osAllocPages(resultId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsAllocPages(resultId, sizeId: sizeId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osWriteStdout(resultId, bufId, lenId) then self.lowerOsWriteStream(resultId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, stream: OsOutputStream.standardOutput, dstRef: dstRef)
			rdataAddr(resultId, label) then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LeaRdata(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), label: label))
			tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, callee, args) then self.lowerTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, callee: callee, args: args, dstRef: dstRef)
			errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) then self.lowerErrorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, dstRef: dstRef, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			osExit(codeId) then self.lowerOsExit(codeId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// P1.5-A1: a function's address (ADRP+ADD via `arm64LeaFuncAddr`), and a call THROUGH a function
			// value (`blr` via `arm64BranchLinkReg`). Both halves are COMPLETE on arm64 and both encoders are
			// real: `emitArm64TargetOp`'s `arm64BranchLinkReg` arm emits `Arm64OpBlr`, and
			// `arm64EmitLeaFuncAddr` emits the mandated ADRP+ADD pair, filing ONE `Arm64FuncAdrpReloc` at the
			// ADRP that `arm64ResolveFuncAdrpRelocs` resolves from the function-label map and BOTH arm64
			// writers patch against `.text`'s VMADDR (`patchArm64FuncAdrpRelocs`, called from `MachOWriter`
			// and `ElfWriter` alike). That reloc IS the code→`.text` channel an earlier note here called
			// missing, and calling it missing is what kept A1's cases gated off arm64 long after they ran:
			// a function value and a capturing closure both execute natively, so those cases carry no target
			// marker and are pinned by arm64 goldens like every other target's.
			funcAddr(resultId, funcName) then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LeaFuncAddr(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), funcName: funcName))
			callIndirect(resultId, calleeReg, resultType, argFloatMask, args) then self.lowerCallIndirect(resultId, calleeReg: calleeReg, args: args, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
			osPanic(label) then self.lowerPanic(label, dstRef: dstRef)
			// P1.5 mid-body-yield: the green-thread sleep substrate (`osReadClock`/`osSleepMs`) is x64-windows
			// only at this rung — its QueryPerformanceCounter clock and Windows `Sleep` have no arm64 primitive
			// yet, and a `sleep` program is spec-gated to x64-windows, so neither op ever reaches arm64 lowering.
			osReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId) then self.lowerOsReadClock(ticksPtrId, freqPtrId: freqPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSleepMs(msId) then self.lowerOsSleepMs(msId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC8: THE TWO OPS THAT SPAWN AND REAP A CHILD LOWER HERE.** Both go through
			// `Arm64DarwinRuntime` chunks, for the structural reason every out-param op in this file does: the
			// PROCESS_INFORMATION and the exit-code word must be written after a call that answers in a
			// register. `osProcessSpawn` and `osProcessSpawnInherit` share ONE chunk, because the flag that
			// separates them on Windows — `bInheritHandles` — has no POSIX twin: `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT`
			// plus the file actions decide what crosses the exec, whichever op asked.
			osProcessSpawn(commandId, cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId, startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId, envPtrId) then self.lowerOsProcessSpawn(commandId, cwdPtrId: cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId: creationFlagsId, startupPtrId: startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtrId, envPtrId: envPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osProcessExitCode(handleId, codePtrId) then self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostProcExitCodeName, args: twoArgs(handleId, second: codePtrId))
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC3: THE THREE WAIT/HANDLE OPS DO LOWER HERE, AND THEY HAD TO — they are STATICALLY
			// REACHABLE in a pure-`async` program.** The netpoll's process arm and `__gt_proc_check` sit on
			// paths the dead-function prune keeps, so the isel sees them even though the proc store they read
			// is provably empty on a lane where nothing can spawn a child. `osWaitHandle` is exercised for
			// real, by the worker park and the join; the other two are lowered so the compile succeeds and are
			// never executed.
			osWaitHandles(handlesBase, countId, timeoutMsId) then self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostWaitAnyName, args: threeArgs(handlesBase, second: countId, third: timeoutMsId))
			osCloseHandle(handleId) then self.lowerOsCloseHandle(handleId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osWaitHandle(resultId, handleId, timeoutMsId) then self.lowerOsWaitHandle(resultId, handleId: handleId, timeoutMsId: timeoutMsId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// F1a + F1c: the four PAGE-LIFECYCLE ops all lower here — `munmap`, `madvise`, and two spellings of
			// `mmap`. They are the one OS family on this backend that is NOT x64-windows-gated, and `osAllocPages`
			// above is why: the allocator's page SOURCE already runs on every lane, so its free, decommit, reserve
			// and commit twins must too.
			osFreePages(addrId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsFreePages(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osDecommitPages(addrId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsDecommitPages(addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// F1b: the byte fill's size ladder — two pre-moves and the macro-op that IS the dispatch.
			memFill(destId, byteCountId, value) then self.lowerMemFill(destId, byteCountId: byteCountId, value: value, dstRef: dstRef)
			osCommitPages(resultId, addrId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsCommitPages(resultId, addrId: addrId, sizeId: sizeId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osReservePages(resultId, sizeId) then self.lowerOsReservePages(resultId, sizeId: sizeId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// The dense-match jump table is ALWAYS a block terminator, never a body op — see `lowerTerminatorOp`.
			switch then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `switch` op appeared as a body op (lowering bug — the jump table is always a block terminator)")
			// P1.7a slice 2b-iv-C: constrained-type-parameter dispatch, the third and last target to get it.
			// Both writers now fill the witness table's `funcAbs64InRdata` slots — ELF bakes an absolute `.text`
			// VA (the image is static/ET_EXEC, so the x64 bake is CPU-invariant) and Mach-O threads each slot
			// into its dyld chained-fixup chain as a PIE rebase, the same channel a String `buffer@0` rides.
			witnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) then self.lowerWitnessCall(resultId, witnessAddr: witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
			witnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId, witnessAddr, methodIndex, resultType, argFloatMask, args) then self.lowerWitnessTryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, witnessAddr: witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, resultType: resultType, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐ **MAC8: A PIPE IS A PIPE; A COMPLETION PORT IS NOT.** `osPipeCreate`'s name, security
			// attributes and port operands are Windows shapes with nothing to bind to here — see
			// `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinPipeChunk` — so only the two out-params are passed on.
			osPipeCreate(_, _, _, readPtrId, writePtrId) then self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostPipeName, args: twoArgs(readPtrId, second: writePtrId))
			// ⛔ **`osIocpCreate` CANNOT BE EMITTED ON THIS LANE, AND THAT IS A BUILD-TIME FACT RATHER THAN A
			// HOPE.** Its ONE producer is `GtRuntime.buildIoInit`, which `installGtRuntime` builds only under
			// the `completionPort` half of `IoCompletionShape` — and this lane answers `inlineCompletion`, where
			// a read completes in its caller and there is no port, no wake event and no drain thread to create.
			// So this is a compile-time panic and not a runtime one: reaching it means the shape fork was
			// bypassed, which is a compiler bug rather than anything a program can ask for.
			osIocpCreate then panic("lowerStdToArm64: `osIocpCreate` reached the arm64 isel — a completion port is emitted ONLY by `GtRuntime.buildIoInit`, which is built under `IoCompletionShape.completionPort`, and every arm64 lane answers `inlineCompletion` (a read completes in its caller, so there is nothing to poll and nothing to wake). Reaching here means the IO-completion shape fork was bypassed")
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC3: the P/M tier's own five ops, which sit INSIDE the IOCP band by declaration order and
			// have nothing to do with IOCP.** `__sched_init_procs` builds them unconditionally under `usesGt`,
			// so every one is reachable in a plain `async` program: the run-queue lock, each MACHINE's own
			// park event (it was each P's wake event until W213-C2) and a worker M's birth. See
			// `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s `DarwinWaitObject` for why an event is a condition variable here and why
			// one object serves both kinds of handle.
			osEventCreate(resultId) then self.lowerOsEventCreate(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtrId, argId) then self.lowerOsThreadCreate(resultId, entryPtrId: entryPtrId, argId: argId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osLockInit(lockPtrId) then self.lowerOsLockInit(lockPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osLockEnter(lockPtrId) then self.lowerOsLockImport(lockPtrId, importSlot: OsImportSlot.pthreadMutexLock, chunk: LinuxLockEnterName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osLockLeave(lockPtrId) then self.lowerOsLockImport(lockPtrId, importSlot: OsImportSlot.pthreadMutexUnlock, chunk: LinuxLockLeaveName, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC8: AN "OVERLAPPED" READ ON THIS LANE IS A PLAIN `read`, AND ITS OVERLAPPED OPERAND IS
			// DEAD.** The op's contract is *"answer TRUE with the count in `bytesOutPtr` if the read completed
			// SYNCHRONOUSLY, FALSE with `ERROR_IO_PENDING` if it is in flight"*. A blocking descriptor read
			// completes synchronously by definition, so this lane only ever takes the first arm —
			// `__gt_subp_yield_read`'s park arm is unreachable here, which is exactly what lets the whole IOCP
			// substrate be absent. It is `osReadFile`'s chunk verbatim, because it is the same four arguments
			// and the same answer.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE READ ENDS ARE LEFT BLOCKING ON PURPOSE.** A non-blocking read would answer `EAGAIN` where
			// the graph reads 0 as EOF, and `buildSubpReadLine` reads WITHOUT peeking first — so a spurious
			// EAGAIN would latch a false end-of-stream. The cost is that the streaming line reader blocks its M
			// until the child writes or exits; the COLLECT loop never blocks, because `osPipePeek` has already
			// said bytes are there. Lifting that would need a kqueue netpoll to park on, which is its own rung.
			osReadOverlapped(resultId, handleId, bufId, capId, bytesOutPtrId, _) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: fourArgs(handleId, second: bufId, third: capId, fourth: bytesOutPtrId), chunk: HostReadFileName, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC4: `GetLastError` SITS INSIDE THE IOCP BAND BY DECLARATION ORDER AND BELONGS TO THE
			// FILESYSTEM.** It is the op every failing file and directory call is classified through, so it
			// lands here with them rather than with the overlapped reads it was declared beside.
			osLastError(resultId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: ValueIdArray.create(), chunk: HostLastErrorName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osProcessSpawnInherit(commandId, cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId, startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId, envPtrId) then self.lowerOsProcessSpawn(commandId, cwdPtrId: cwdPtrId, creationFlagsId: creationFlagsId, startupPtrId: startupPtrId, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtrId, envPtrId: envPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⛔ **`osCancelIo` IS STATICALLY REACHABLE AND DYNAMICALLY UNREACHABLE HERE, so it is a RUNTIME
			// abort rather than a compile-time one** — `osThreadCreate`'s shape on the Linux lane, and the same
			// argument.
			// `__gt_promise_drop`'s cancel arm is built whenever the pipe substrate is, but it runs only when
			// the dropped promise's green thread has `ioParked` set, and nothing sets that on a lane where
			// every read completes in its caller. A silent no-op would turn "this promise was dropped mid-read"
			// into a read nobody cancelled and a handle nobody closed.
			osCancelIo then self.lowerRuntimeAbort(dstRef, message: Arm64NoCancelIoMessage)
			// ⭐ MAC2: the PROCESS ID. It sits inside the IOCP band because the pipe-name uniquifier minted it
			// (`GtRuntime.emitGtPipeNameUniquifier`), but the question it asks is the OS's and not the
			// scheduler's — `getpid()` here, `GetCurrentProcessId()` there.
			osGetPid(resultId) then self.lowerOsGetPid(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// MAC8: the same `pipe` as its inbound twin. Win32 needs two calls because one end must be a
			// SYNCHRONOUS handle and the other an overlapped one; POSIX makes no such distinction, so the two
			// ops share one chunk and differ only in which out-param is which end.
			osPipeCreateOutbound(_, _, childReadPtrId, parentWritePtrId) then self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostPipeName, args: twoArgs(childReadPtrId, second: parentWritePtrId))
			// ⭐ MAC4: `WriteFile` is a `write` here, and it is the SAME chunk shape `osReadFile` takes one band
			// down — a descriptor, a buffer, a length and the out-param the count is published through.
			osWriteFile(resultId, handleId, bufId, lenId, bytesOutPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: fourArgs(handleId, second: bufId, third: lenId, fourth: bytesOutPtrId), chunk: HostWriteFileName, dstRef: dstRef)
			// The `__Builtins` WALL clock reads `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime`, a Win32 import with no arm64
			// counterpart at this rung; SemanticCheck refuses such a program at its source span (E3104), so
			// this is the backstop for a compiler bug rather than the user-facing rejection.
			osReadWallClock(ftPtrId) then self.lowerOsReadWallClock(ftPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC4/5: THE WHOLE FILE AND DIRECTORY BAND LOWERS HERE.** This arm was one range and one
			// panic — *"file IO is x64-windows-gated at R4.1/R4.2"* — and the ruling it named is discharged on
			// this lane rather than deferred. Two shapes: the three that are a bare libSystem call with nothing
			// to outlive it lower INLINE, and the rest branch to a chunk in `Arm64DarwinRuntime`, for that
			// file's structural reason (an out-param must survive a call that answers in a register).
			osFileOpenRead(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileOpen(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, importSlot: OsImportSlot.open, darwinArgument: PosixOpenReadOnly, linuxFlags: PosixOpenReadOnly, linuxMode: PosixNoFileMode as MachineWord, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileClose(handleId) then self.lowerOsFileClose(handleId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osReadFile(resultId, handleId, bufId, lenId, bytesOutPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: fourArgs(handleId, second: bufId, third: lenId, fourth: bytesOutPtrId), chunk: HostReadFileName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileOpenWrite(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileOpen(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, importSlot: OsImportSlot.creat, darwinArgument: PosixCreateFileMode, linuxFlags: LinuxOpenCreateTruncateFlags, linuxMode: PosixCreateFileMode as MachineWord, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileSize(resultId, handleId, sizeOutPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(handleId, second: sizeOutPtrId), chunk: HostFileSizeName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileDelete(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: argsOf(pathPtrId), chunk: HostFileDeleteName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileRename(resultId, oldPathPtrId, newPathPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(oldPathPtrId, second: newPathPtrId), chunk: HostFileRenameName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileFindFirst(resultId, pathPtrId, findDataPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(pathPtrId, second: findDataPtrId), chunk: HostDirFindFirstName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileFindClose(handleId) then self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostDirFindCloseName, args: argsOf(handleId))
			osFileFindNext(resultId, handleId, findDataPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(handleId, second: findDataPtrId), chunk: HostDirFindNextName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osDirCreate(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: argsOf(pathPtrId), chunk: HostDirCreateName, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⚠ **THE OP'S FIELD ORDER IS NOT THE C ONE**, exactly as `lowerOsExecutablePath` records for its
			// twin: the op reads `(result, bufLen, bufPtr)` and `getcwd` takes the BUFFER first.
			osDirCurrentPath(resultId, bufLenId, bufPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(bufPtrId, second: bufLenId), chunk: HostDirCurrentPathName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osFileAttributes(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: argsOf(pathPtrId), chunk: HostFileAttributesName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osPanicValue(messagePtrId) then self.lowerPanicValue(messagePtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// The command line is x64-windows only: `GetCommandLineA` is a Win32 import, and macOS hands argv to
			// `mrt_start` in x0/x1 instead — a DIFFERENT SHAPE (a pre-split vector, not one string to split), so a
			// lowering here would have to answer a question this op does not ask. SemanticCheck refuses such a
			// program at its source span (E3104, `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`'s `__cl_` prefix), so this is the
			// backstop for a compiler bug rather than the user-facing rejection.
			osCommandLine then panic("lowerStdToArm64: `osCommandLine` asks for ONE UNSPLIT COMMAND LINE, which is a Windows shape and not a missing lowering — MAC7 serves argv on this lane through a DIFFERENT Std graph (`CommandLineRuntime.commandLineShape`), which reads the vector `mrt_start` captured and never emits this op. Reaching here means the unsplit-string graph was installed for a target whose process start-up does not have one")
			// W5's two ops, and they now go OPPOSITE WAYS — which is exactly what the shared paragraph they used
			// to carry could not express. ⛔ It read "Both are x64-windows only … Serving them is a rung, not a
			// lowering", and half of that stopped being true at MAC2; a claim about two ops is a claim that can
			// only be half-corrected.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC6: `osStdHandle` LOWERS, AND THE PARAGRAPH THAT REFUSED IT OVERSTATED THE PROBLEM.** It
			// read: *"there is no `GetStdHandle` at all under POSIX — fd 0 IS the handle, so the op would have
			// no work to do and its callers would want a different graph … a rung rather than a lowering."*
			// The first half is right and the conclusion does not follow: an op with no work to do is an op
			// whose lowering is the IDENTITY on the value, and the three selectors map onto the three
			// descriptors by one subtraction. The graph above it is untouched, and `SubprocessRuntime`'s
			// `inherit` stdio kind gets the right descriptors for free the day that band lands here.
			osStdHandle(resultId, whichId) then self.lowerOsStdHandle(resultId, whichId: whichId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐ MAC2: the executable path DOES lower here, through a chunk. The three platforms genuinely
			// disagree (`GetModuleFileNameA`, `_NSGetExecutablePath` + `realpath`, `/proc/self/exe` —
			// `stdlib/Process.maxon:26-27` names all three), and that disagreement is DISCHARGED on this lane
			// rather than left as a refusal: `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinExePathChunk` writes Darwin's
			// protocol out, and the target-neutral doubling loop above it is untouched.
			osExecutablePath(resultId, bufLenId, bufPtrId) then self.lowerOsExecutablePath(resultId, bufLenId: bufLenId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// W35: the same graph as the stdout write, one descriptor over. Unlike its W5 neighbours above this
			// is NOT x64-windows-gated — a POSIX `write` serves both streams, so `printError` runs here.
			osWriteStderr(resultId, bufId, lenId) then self.lowerOsWriteStream(resultId, bufId: bufId, lenId: lenId, stream: OsOutputStream.standardError, dstRef: dstRef)
			// A promoted record's address — `add dest, x29, #(16 + slot*8)`, the frame-slot mirror of
			// `arm64LeaGlobal`. arm64 has no per-function slot base (`Arm64Backend.arm64SlotDisplacement`), so
			// the encoder resolves it from the slot alone, exactly as it does a spill.
			stackRecordAddr(resultId, slot) then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.leaRegSlot(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), slot: slot))
			// ⭐⭐ **THE PRODUCER'S ATTACH — READ THE VARIABLE, THEN OPEN WHAT IT NAMES, IN ONE CHUNK.** Win32
			// fuses `GetEnvironmentVariableA` with `OpenFileMappingA` because the first FILLS a buffer the
			// second reads, and arm64-Linux keeps that shape; macOS's `getenv` answers a pointer into the
			// environment block that `shm_open` takes directly, so the buffer and its length have no reader on
			// THAT route alone. Both operands travel here because the op serves both.
			osOpenNamedSegment(resultId, namePtrId, bufPtrId, bufLenId) then self.lowerNamedSegmentAttach(resultId, namePtrId: namePtrId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, bufLenId: bufLenId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// The mapping takes the descriptor whichever call minted it, exactly as `MapViewOfFile` takes a
			// section handle from either the open or the create. ONE chunk on both lanes — an `mmap` differs
			// only in how it reaches the kernel.
			osMapNamedSegment(resultId, handleId, sizeId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(handleId, second: sizeId), chunk: HostShmMapName, dstRef: dstRef)
			// MAC8: `GetTickCount64` — the same `CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW` the scheduler's own clock reads, in
			// milliseconds. It sits in the DebugStream band by declaration order and belongs to the subprocess
			// family, whose deadlines are the only thing that measures with it.
			osTickCountMs(resultId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: ValueIdArray.create(), chunk: HostTickMsName, dstRef: dstRef)
			// The consumer's half. The open only NAMES, so the chunk sizes what it opened with `ftruncate` and
			// undoes the name if that fails — which is why a create is a chunk here and one call on Win32.
			osCreateNamedSegment(resultId, namePtrId, sizeId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(namePtrId, second: sizeId), chunk: self.namedSegmentCreateChunk(), dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⚠ The LENGTH is read here and nowhere else in the tree: `munmap` takes one where `UnmapViewOfFile`
			// derives it from the base (`StdOp.osUnmapNamedSegment`).
			osUnmapNamedSegment(baseId, sizeId) then self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostShmUnmapName, args: twoArgs(baseId, second: sizeId))
			// ⭐ **THE STEP WIN32 GETS FOR FREE.** A POSIX shared object outlives every descriptor and every
			// mapping until its NAME is removed, so the consumer's teardown ends here.
			osUnpublishNamedSegment(namePtrId) then self.lowerOsUnpublishNamedSegment(namePtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⛔ The four attached-subprocess host primitives are x64-windows only on the same terms as the
			// spawn/wait/pipe band above, and `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses a program that
			// reaches one at its own source span (E3104) — so this is the backstop for a compiler bug that got
			// past that gate, never the user-facing rejection.
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC8: `osHandleSetInherit` EMITS NOTHING HERE, AND THE REASON IS THAT DARWIN'S DEFAULT IS
			// INVERTED.** On Windows a handle must be MARKED inheritable before `bInheritHandles=TRUE` will
			// pass it to a child; here the spawn sets `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT`, under which NOTHING
			// crosses the exec except the descriptors the file actions name — so there is no per-handle mark to
			// make, and marking one would be marking it for a mechanism this lane does not use. It is a stated
			// no-op rather than a silent one: an op whose whole meaning is discharged by another op's flag.
			osHandleSetInherit then break 'lower'
			osPipePeek(resultId, handleId, availPtrId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: twoArgs(handleId, second: availPtrId), chunk: HostPipePeekName, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐ MAC3: the ENVIRONMENT READ sits inside the attached-subprocess band by declaration order and is
			// not a subprocess op at all — `__sched_init_procs` reads `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` through it on every
			// green-thread program. `getenv` answers a POINTER where `GetEnvironmentVariableA` fills a buffer,
			// so the copy is the chunk's work; see `buildArm64DarwinEnvReadChunk`.
			osEnvRead(resultId, namePtrId, bufPtrId, bufLenId) then self.lowerOsEnvRead(resultId, namePtrId: namePtrId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, bufLenId: bufLenId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osProcessTerminate(handleId, exitCodeId) then self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostProcTerminateName, args: twoArgs(handleId, second: exitCodeId))
			// ⭐⭐ **THE SOCKET BAND — SEVEN OPS, AND ONE OF THEM REACHES NO KERNEL ON EITHER LANE.** Winsock
			// and BSD sockets are the same five verbs (`socket`/`connect`/`send`/`recv`/`close`) plus a
			// rendezvous only Windows has, so `osSocketStartup` lowers to its own answer rather than to a call
			// (see `lowerOsSocketStartup`).
			//
			// ⛔ **`osSocketResolve` AND `osSocketFreeAddrInfo` REACH THIS FILE ON macOS ONLY, AND THE LINUX
			// REFUSAL IS NOT A MISSING LOWERING.** `getaddrinfo` is a libc FUNCTION that reads
			// `/etc/nsswitch.conf` and speaks DNS; a raw static image has nothing to call, so the Linux lanes
			// run a DIFFERENT Std graph — `ManagedSocketRuntime`'s own resolver, which emits neither op — and
			// each arm states that rather than pretending to a lowering it cannot have.
			osSocketStartup(resultId, _) then self.lowerOsSocketStartup(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSocketResolve(resultId, hostPtrId, hintsPtrId, outPtrId) then self.lowerOsSocketResolve(resultId, hostPtrId: hostPtrId, hintsPtrId: hintsPtrId, outPtrId: outPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtrId) then self.lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSocketCreate(resultId) then self.lowerOsSocketCreate(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSocketConnect(resultId, sockId, addrPtrId) then self.lowerOsSocketConnect(resultId, sockId: sockId, addrPtrId: addrPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSocketSend(resultId, sockId, bufPtrId, lenId) then self.lowerOsSocketTransfer(resultId, sockId: sockId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, lenId: lenId, importSlot: OsImportSlot.send, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallSendto, suppressSigpipe: true, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSocketRecv(resultId, sockId, bufPtrId, lenId) then self.lowerOsSocketTransfer(resultId, sockId: sockId, bufPtrId: bufPtrId, lenId: lenId, importSlot: OsImportSlot.recv, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallRecvfrom, suppressSigpipe: false, dstRef: dstRef)
			// A socket is a DESCRIPTOR on both lanes, so this is `lowerOsFileClose` one family over and reaches
			// the same primitive — `closesocket` exists only because a Win32 `SOCKET` is not a file `HANDLE`.
			osSocketClose(sockId) then self.lowerOsFileClose(sockId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐ The MACHINE query: `GetActiveProcessorCount` on Windows, `sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)`
			// here. Two APIs with two failure conventions and one question — see `lowerOsCpuCount`.
			osCpuCount(resultId) then self.lowerOsCpuCount(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐ The thread-CPU read, through a CHUNK: `QueryThreadCycleTime` writes its answer through the
			// caller's pointer while `clock_gettime_nsec_np` returns it in x0, so the pointer must outlive the
			// call and an isel cannot hold it there (`Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s header carries the argument).
			osThreadCpuTicks(outPtrId) then self.lowerOsThreadCpuTicks(outPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// F2: the atomics and the per-thread identity word. Neither atomic is OS-routed — LDAXR/STLXR are ISA
			// facts — while the token IS, because only one of the two OSes here has a thread-pointer register a raw
			// static binary may read (see `lowerCurrentThreadToken`).
			atomicCas(resultId, addrId, expectedId, replacementId) then self.lowerAtomicCas(resultId, addrId: addrId, expectedId: expectedId, replacementId: replacementId, dstRef: dstRef)
			atomicRmw(resultId, addrId, operandId, opcode) then self.lowerAtomicRmw(resultId, addrId: addrId, operandId: operandId, opcode: opcode, dstRef: dstRef)
			currentThreadToken(resultId) then self.lowerCurrentThreadToken(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// G1: the multi-M substrate is x64-windows only, exactly like the IOCP ops above it — `TlsAlloc` /
			// `TlsSetValue` / the `gs:`-relative slot read / `SetEvent` are Win32 shapes with no AAPCS64 analogue
			// (Darwin's is `pthread_getspecific` + a libc this lane does not link), so a lowering here would be
			// untested dead code no test could reach.
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC3: the per-OS-thread slot and the event signal. The old note here said a lowering would
			// be "untested dead code no test could reach" and named `pthread_getspecific` + "a libc this lane
			// does not link"; BOTH halves have stopped being true.** arm64-macOS links libSystem for every
			// other host family already, and the READ needs no libc call at all — Darwin's TSD array is at the
			// masked thread pointer, so `pthread_getspecific(key)` IS a base-plus-index load
			// (`lowerTlsSlotLoad` carries the measurement). What remains x64-windows-only is the LINUX route,
			// which each of these arms refuses on its own terms.
			tlsAlloc(resultId) then self.lowerTlsAlloc(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			tlsSetValue(indexId, valueId) then self.lowerTlsSetValue(indexId, valueId: valueId, dstRef: dstRef)
			tlsSlotLoad(resultId, tebOffsetId) then self.lowerTlsSlotLoad(resultId, tebOffsetId: tebOffsetId, dstRef: dstRef)
			osSetEvent(handleId) then self.lowerOsSetEvent(handleId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⚠ ITS OWN ARM, NOT THE SCHEDULER'S, and that separation is what let MAC2 land it while the multi-M
			// substrate above stays refused: `SetPriorityClass`/`GetPriorityClass` on Windows,
			// `setpriority`/`getpriority` here. ⭐ The THREAD-INHERITANCE half of the old note is DISCHARGED on
			// this lane rather than deferred — Darwin scopes nice to the PROCESS — and still stands for Linux,
			// which scopes it to the THREAD; `PosixPrioProcess` carries that asymmetry.
			osEnterBackgroundPriority(resultId) then self.lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriority(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// MAC4: `openWriteExecutable` is `openWrite` with the execute bits — the same `creat`, a different
			// mode. On Windows the two are one `CreateFileA`; here the mode is the whole difference, which is
			// why they are two ops (`StdOp.osFileOpenWriteExecutable`).
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable(resultId, pathPtrId) then self.lowerOsFileOpen(resultId, pathPtrId: pathPtrId, importSlot: OsImportSlot.creat, darwinArgument: PosixCreateExecutableFileMode, linuxFlags: LinuxOpenCreateTruncateFlags, linuxMode: PosixCreateExecutableFileMode as MachineWord, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⭐ **`osEnvBlock` IS SERVED HERE, AND THE PANIC THAT USED TO STAND IN ITS PLACE RESTED ON A FALSE
			// PREMISE.** It claimed the whole attached-subprocess substrate was refused on this lane; the
			// facility table says `subprocess gives true` for arm64-macOS, so `buildSubpEnvEntry` was built,
			// nothing dropped it, and every program that so much as named `runConfiguration` panicked the
			// compiler — with the DEFAULT `Environment.inherit`, which reads no environment at all.
			osEnvBlock(resultId) then self.lowerOsEnvBlock(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			// ⛔ `osEnvBlockFree` HAS NO COUNTERPART ON EITHER POSIX LANE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS A FACT ABOUT
			// `environ` RATHER THAN A GAP: both answer `EnvironmentSnapshotShape.pointerVector`, whose producer
			// emits no free because the vector is the live table and not a copy. A no-op stub would be the same
			// claim written where nothing can check it; this panic is the backstop for a producer that starts
			// emitting one.
			// ⭐ **`GetFileType` THROUGH A CHUNK ON BOTH ARM64 LANES, AND THE TWO BODIES ARE DIFFERENT CALLS.**
			// Darwin's is `isatty` plus a narrowing; Linux's is the `ioctl(fd, TCGETS, …)` `isatty` is made of,
			// which needs a frame for the `struct termios` the kernel fills. Either way the answer is mapped
			// into the Win32 word this tier reads (`StdOp.osHandleFileType` carries why it speaks Win32), so
			// the lowering is one unconditional chunk call and only the appended body differs.
			osHandleFileType(resultId, handleId) then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: argsOf(handleId), chunk: HostHandleFileTypeName, dstRef: dstRef)
			osEnvBlockFree then panic("lowerStdToArm64: `osEnvBlockFree` releases a `GetEnvironmentStringsA` COPY and neither POSIX lane has one — `SubprocessRuntime.environmentSnapshotShape` answers `pointerVector` for both, whose `__gt_subp_env_entry` body indexes the live environment vector and emits no release. Reaching here means a producer emitted one anyway, which is a compiler bug rather than a user-facing rejection")
		end 'lower'

		if bracketed 'readTheVerdict'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(SchedExitSyscallName))
		end 'readTheVerdict'
	end 'lowerBodyOp'

	function lowerTerminatorOp(op StdOp, dstRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType)
		match op 'lower'
			ret(retValId) then self.lowerReturn(retValId, dstRef: dstRef, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			retVoid then self.lowerVoidReturn(dstRef, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) then self.lowerCondBranch(condId, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, dstRef: dstRef)
			branch(targetBlockId) then self.emitTerminator(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Branch(targetBlockId))
			const then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `const` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			binOp then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `binOp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			unaryOp then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `unaryOp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			cmp then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `cmp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			binOpImm then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `binOpImm` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			cmpImm then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `cmpImm` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			div then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `div` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			mod then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `mod` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			param then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `param` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			call then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `call` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			globalAddr to storeIndirect then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a memory-band op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			osAllocPages then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osAllocPages` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			osWriteStdout then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osWriteStdout` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			rdataAddr then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `rdataAddr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug)")
			tryCall then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `tryCall` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a throwing call is a body op)")
			errorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId) then self.lowerErrorReturn(retValId, errorFlagId: errorFlagId, dstRef: dstRef, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			osExit then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osExit` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a process exit is a body op)")
			funcAddr then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `funcAddr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a function address is a value-producing body op)")
			callIndirect then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `callIndirect` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an indirect call is a value-producing body op)")
			witnessCall then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `witnessCall` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a witness call is a value-producing body op)")
			witnessTryCall then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `witnessTryCall` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a throwing witness call is a value-producing body op)")
			osPanic then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osPanic` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a range panic is a body op followed by a dead return)")
			osReadClock then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osReadClock` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a clock read is a value-consuming body op)")
			osSleepMs then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osSleepMs` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an OS sleep is a body op)")
			osProcessSpawn to osWaitHandle then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a P1.5 subprocess op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — spawn/wait/exit-code/close are all body ops)")
			osFreePages then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osFreePages` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a page release is a body op)")
			// The dense-match jump table lowers to the arm64 jump-table target op, registering its `.rdata`
			// slot blob (its label the ADRP+ADD addresses and the writer patches). Routed through a helper so
			// the `denseTargetBlockIds` Array is not bound in this big match.
			switch then self.lowerSwitch(op, dstRef: dstRef)
			osPipeCreate to osCancelIo then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a P1.5 IOCP op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — all are body ops)")
			osGetPid then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osGetPid` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a process-id read is a value-producing body op)")
			osPipeCreateOutbound to osWriteFile then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a P1.5 streaming-stdin op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — both are body ops)")
			osReadWallClock then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osReadWallClock` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a wall-clock read is a value-consuming body op)")
			osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a file-IO op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — every one is a body op)")
			osPanicValue then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osPanicValue` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a rendered panic is a body op followed by a dead return, exactly like `osPanic`)")
			osCommandLine then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osCommandLine` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a command-line read is a value-consuming body op)")
			osStdHandle then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osStdHandle` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a standard-handle read is a value-producing body op)")
			osExecutablePath then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osExecutablePath` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an executable-path read is a value-producing body op)")
			osWriteStderr then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osWriteStderr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a stderr write is a body op, exactly like its stdout twin)")
			stackRecordAddr then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `stackRecordAddr` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a frame address is a value-producing body op)")
			osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a DebugStream host primitive appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — all three are value-producing body ops)")
			osHandleSetInherit to osProcessTerminate then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an attached-subprocess host primitive appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — the inherit mark, the pipe peek, the env read and the terminate are all body ops)")
			osSocketStartup to osSocketClose then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a socket host primitive appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — every socket primitive is a body op, never a terminator)")
			osCpuCount then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osCpuCount` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a machine query is a value-producing body op)")
			osThreadCpuTicks then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osThreadCpuTicks` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a thread-CPU read is a body op writing through an out-param)")
			// F1a: a decommit is a body op, exactly as `osFreePages` above is — a page-lifecycle op transfers
			// control nowhere.
			osDecommitPages then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osDecommitPages` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a decommit is a body op, like the page release above)")
			// F1c: a reserve and a commit are value-producing body ops, for the decommit's reason.
			osCommitPages to osReservePages then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a page reserve or commit appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — both are value-producing body ops, like the page allocation above)")
			memFill then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `memFill` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a byte fill is a body op; its size dispatch branches INSIDE one macro-op's encoder, never between IR blocks)")
			// F2: all three are value-producing body ops. The CAS/RMW retry loop branches, but inside one macro-op's
			// encoder — never between IR blocks, exactly as the fill ladder above.
			atomicCas to currentThreadToken then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an atomic or a thread-token read appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — all three are value-producing body ops)")
			// G1: none of the four terminates a block; two produce a value and two are void side effects.
			tlsAlloc to osSetEvent then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a TLS op or an event signal appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — neither is a terminator)")
			osEnterBackgroundPriority then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osEnterBackgroundPriority` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — the priority door is a value-producing body op)")
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an `osFileOpenWriteExecutable` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an open is a value-producing body op, exactly like its two siblings)")
			osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an environment-block read or release appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — one produces a value and the other is a void side effect, and neither terminates)")
			loadAcquire or storeRelease then panic("lowerStdToArm64: an ordered load or store appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — both are body ops)")
			osHandleFileType then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a file-type query appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — `osHandleFileType` produces a value and does not terminate)")
			osCreateNamedSegment to osUnpublishNamedSegment then panic("lowerStdToArm64: a named-section create or unmap appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — one produces a value and the other is a void side effect, and neither terminates)")
		end 'lower'
	end 'lowerTerminatorOp'

	// Lower the dense-match jump table to the arm64 `arm64JumpTable` terminator — the twin of x64's
	// `emitJumpTable`. Reserves the `.rdata` blob of N i32 slots HERE (the lowering owns `globalData`,
	// exactly as `lowerFloatConst` registers its float island); the op carries the unique blob label the
	// ADRP+ADD pair addresses and the writer patches. RE-MATCHES `op` in a SMALL match, the same discipline
	// x64 uses.
	function lowerSwitch(op StdOp, dstRef BlockRef)
		match op 'sw'
			switch(subjectId, denseTargetBlockIds, defaultBlockId) then self.emitJumpTable(subjectId, caseTargets: denseTargetBlockIds, defaultTarget: defaultBlockId, dstRef: dstRef)
			default panic("lowerSwitch: expected a `switch` op, got a different StdOp variant — `lowerTerminatorOp` routes only `switch` here")
		end 'sw'
	end 'lowerSwitch'

	function emitJumpTable(subjectId ValueId, caseTargets BlockIdArray, defaultTarget BlockId, dstRef BlockRef)
		let tableLabel = self.globalData.registerJumpTable(caseTargets.count())
		self.emitTerminator(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64JumpTable(TargetVReg.virtual(subjectId), defaultTarget: defaultTarget, caseTargets: caseTargets, tableLabel: tableLabel.toByteArray()))
	end 'emitJumpTable'

	// Materialize a constant. The `movz`/`movk` ladder is the encoder's; the isel emits one op.
	function lowerConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt, valueType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(valueType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatConst(resultId, value: value, valueType: valueType, dstRef: dstRef)
			return
		end 'float'
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), value: value as MachineWord))
	end 'lowerConst'

	// Materialize a FLOAT constant: register its IEEE bits as a `.rdata` island and load them with the
	// ADRP+LDR pair (`ldr d, [pc-rel]`). A constant POOL, not an immediate — there is no `fmov d, imm64`
	// for an arbitrary double.
	//
	// The island's NAME and its eight bytes are both the shared table's
	// (`GlobalDataTable.registerFloatConstant`), not this file's: the width and the serializer are the same
	// fact x64 needs, and this function used to carry its own `Arm64DoubleBytes` spelling of it beside its
	// own copy of the label prefix. What is genuinely arm64's stays — the type check and the load op.
	function lowerFloatConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt, valueType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		arm64RequireScalarDouble(valueType, what: "lowerFloatConst")
		let label = self.globalData.registerFloatConstant(value)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64FpLoadRdata(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), label: label.toByteArray()))
	end 'lowerFloatConst'

	// Lower an integer binary op to its 3-address arm64 instruction. arm64 has no two-address form, so
	// `dest`/`lhs`/`rhs` are independent and no seed copy is ever needed — cleaner than x64.
	function lowerBinOp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatBinOp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstRef: dstRef)
			return
		end 'float'

		// EC16 — this `+` is the second half of a `base + index*scale` element address: NOTHING when its
		// one memory reader carries the whole address, else the single `ADD Xd, Xn, Xm, LSL #k` that
		// replaces the scaling multiply.
		if self.scaledIndexFolds.isIndexedAddress(resultId) 'scaledIndexAddress'
			self.lowerScaledIndexAddress(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			return
		end 'scaledIndexAddress'

		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let lhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		let rhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Add(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			sub then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Sub(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			mul then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Mul(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitAnd then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64And(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitOr then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Orr(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitXor then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Eor(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			shl to shrLogical then self.lowerShiftReg(dest, lhsReg: lhsReg, rhsReg: rhsReg, instr: arm64ShiftInstrOf(opcode), dstRef: dstRef)
			// Integer `binOp(div)` is illegal — integer division traps and is `StdOp.div`; only FLOAT
			// division (which cannot fault) rides the pure arith band. Reaching here is a lowering bug.
			div to max then panic("lowerBinOp: `binOp({opcode.name})` with an INTEGER operand type — all three are FLOAT-ONLY (`stdBinOpcodeIsFloatOnly`). Integer division is `StdOp.div` (it can fault); `min`/`max` have no integer form and their operands are promoted by the parser")
			// ⭐ EC18 — ONE instruction, and no pre-move. x64's `lowerMulHighSigned` needs three ops because
			// its `imul r/m64` reads RAX and writes RDX:RAX; `SMULH` names all three registers, so the
			// magic multiply costs arm64 exactly what an ordinary `MUL` costs.
			mulHighSigned then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Smulh(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerBinOp'

	// EC16 — emit a matched `base + index*scale` address: NOTHING when the one memory op reading it
	// carries the address itself, else the single shifted-register `add` that replaces the separate
	// scaling instruction and the `add` it fed. It is pressure-neutral even in that case — `index` was
	// live to the scaling op and its RESULT live from there to the `add`; now `index` is live to the
	// `add` and there is no intermediate at all.
	function lowerScaledIndexAddress(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		if self.scaledIndexFolds.fusesIntoMemoryOp(resultId) 'carriedByTheAccess'
			return
		end 'carriedByTheAccess'

		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AddLsl(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), base: TargetVReg.virtual(self.scaledIndexFolds.baseOf(resultId)), index: TargetVReg.virtual(self.scaledIndexFolds.indexOf(resultId)), scale: self.scaledIndexFolds.scaleOf(resultId)))
	end 'lowerScaledIndexAddress'

	// Lower a FLOAT binary op to its scalar-double NEON instruction. All four are genuine 3-address ops
	// (arm64 FP has a real 3-operand form), so plain defs, no copy. Order matters for `fsub`/`fdiv`.
	function lowerFloatBinOp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		arm64RequireScalarDouble(operandType, what: "lowerFloatBinOp")
		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let lhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		let rhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fadd(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			sub then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fsub(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			mul then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fmul(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			div then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fdiv(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitAnd to shrLogical then panic("lowerFloatBinOp: {opcode.rawValue} has no float form — the bitwise/shift operators are integer-only and a float operand is rejected at the Maxon tier")
			mulHighSigned then panic("lowerFloatBinOp: `mulHighSigned` is INTEGER-ONLY — it is the high half of a 128-bit integer product and no float form of it exists")
			// ⭐ THE FLOAT BINARY OPCODES THAT ARE NOT ONE INSTRUCTION HERE — `fmin`/`fmax` exist and are
			// deliberately not used, because they compute DIFFERENT functions (see `TargetOp.arm64Fcsel`).
			min to max then self.lowerFloatMinMax(dest, lhsReg: lhsReg, rhsReg: rhsReg, kind: floatMinMaxKindFor(opcode), dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerFloatBinOp'

	// ⭐⭐ `min(a, b)` / `max(a, b)` AS A COMPARE-AND-SELECT, WHICH IS THE LANGUAGE'S DEFINITION SPELLED
	// LITERALLY: `fcmp a, b` then `fcsel dest, a, b, <cond>` — "take `a` if the compare holds, else
	// `b`" (`StdBinOpcode`'s `min`/`max` header). ONE function for both, because the two lowerings
	// differ in the CONDITION and in nothing else: same instruction pair, same operand order, same
	// adjacency requirement.
	//
	// ⭐ THE CONDITION IS THE WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT, and it is DERIVED rather than picked here:
	// `floatMinMaxSelectPred` says which comparison the definition names (`<` for `min`, `>` for
	// `max`), and `arm64FloatCondForPred` — the target's ONE float-compare mapping, the same one a
	// source-level `a < b` goes through — turns it into `mi` and `gt`. So the intrinsics cannot drift
	// from the operators on a NaN, and this function decides nothing about IEEE at all.
	//
	// That makes each pair EXACT rather than approximate: `fcmp` leaves N=0, Z=0, C=1, V=1 on an
	// UNORDERED result, and both `mi` (N==1) and `gt` (Z==0 ∧ N==V) are false there, so a NaN in either
	// operand selects `b`, matching x64's `minsd`/`maxsd`, which return their second source on
	// unordered. Two zeros of opposite sign compare EQUAL (Z=1, N=0, V=0), and both conditions are
	// false on equal, so they likewise select `b`. Neither case needs a correction.
	//
	// The two ops are emitted BACK TO BACK because `fcsel` reads the flags `fcmp` set — the same
	// adjacency `lowerCmp`'s `fcmp` + `cset` already relies on.
	function lowerFloatMinMax(dest TargetVReg, lhsReg TargetVReg, rhsReg TargetVReg, kind FloatMinMaxKind, dstRef BlockRef)
		let cond = arm64FloatCondForPred(floatMinMaxSelectPred(kind))

		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fcmp(lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fcsel(dest, ifTrue: lhsReg, ifFalse: rhsReg, cond: cond))
	end 'lowerFloatMinMax'

	// Variable-count shift: arm64's `lslv`/`asrv`/`lsrv` take the count in an ordinary GPR (no fixed CL
	// like x64), so the count stays a virtual and no register is pinned — 3-address, plain def.
	function lowerShiftReg(dest TargetVReg, lhsReg TargetVReg, rhsReg TargetVReg, instr Arm64ShiftInstr, dstRef BlockRef)
		match instr 'i'
			lsl then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Lslv(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			asr then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Asrv(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			lsr then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Lsrv(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
		end 'i'
	end 'lowerShiftReg'

	// Constant-count shift (the imm8 twin). The cast is CHECKED: `constFitsBinOpImmediate` promised
	// 0..63 for a shift, and this is where that promise is collected.
	function lowerShiftImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32, instr Arm64ShiftInstr, dstRef BlockRef)
		let count = imm as ShiftCount
		match instr 'i'
			lsl then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LslImm(dest, src: src, imm: count))
			asr then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AsrImm(dest, src: src, imm: count))
			lsr then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LsrImm(dest, src: src, imm: count))
		end 'i'
	end 'lowerShiftImm'

	// Lower a Std unary op. All three integer forms are single-instruction on arm64 with an independent
	// destination (arm64 reads the zero register as the other operand of `neg`/`mvn`), so plain defs.
	//   • `neg`        — `neg dest, src` (two's complement).
	//   • `bitNot`     — `mvn dest, src` (one's complement of all 64 bits): `not 5` == -6.
	//   • `logicalNot` — `eor dest, src, #1`: a bool is 0/1, so flipping bit 0 negates it.
	// The two conversions cross files: `siToFp` → `scvtf` (int → f64), `fpToSi` → `fcvtzs` (f64 → int,
	// TRUNCATING toward zero — the `z`, which is why it is right for `trunc()`).
	function lowerUnaryOp(resultId ValueId, operand ValueId, opcode StdUnaryOpcode, operandType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) != stdUnaryOperandIsFloat(opcode) 'domain'
			panic("lowerUnaryOp: no arm64 form of {opcode.name} over Std type {operandType.name} — an opcode's operand DOMAIN is part of the opcode (`stdUnaryOperandIsFloat`); an f64 negate in particular is `fneg` (a sign-bit flip, the `fneg` instruction), never the integer `neg`")
		end 'domain'

		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let src = TargetVReg.virtual(operand)
		match opcode 'op'
			neg then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Neg(dest, src: src))
			bitNot then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Mvn(dest, src: src))
			logicalNot then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64EorImm(dest, src: src, imm: Arm64LogicalNotMask))
			siToFp then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Scvtf(dest, src: src))
			fpToSi then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fcvtzs(dest, src: src))
			// The MATH INTRINSICS: `fsqrt` is its own instruction, and `frintm`/`frintp`/`frintn` are ONE
			// instruction differing only in its `rmode` field. This arm reads the SAME
			// `floatRoundModeFor` the x64 isel does — the rounding is decided once for the language and
			// encoded twice (`arm64FrintOpcode`, `roundsdImm8`), never decided twice.
			floor to round then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Frint(dest, src: src, mode: floatRoundModeFor(opcode)))
			sqrt then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fsqrt(dest, src: src))
			// `fabs` is its own instruction here, which is exactly where arm64 and x64 part company on
			// this opcode: x64 has to synthesize the sign-bit clear (`TargetOp.absF64RegReg`), arm64 asks
			// the hardware for it.
			abs then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fabs(dest, src: src))
			// `fneg` is its own instruction here too — the sign-bit FLIP beside `fabs`'s clear, and again
			// where x64 has to synthesize (`TargetOp.negF64RegReg`) and arm64 asks the hardware.
			fneg then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fneg(dest, src: src))
			// The BIT REINTERPRETATION (`float.hash`) — `FMOV Xd, Dn`, the general↔vector transfer
			// form, NOT the `FMOV Dd, Dn` the shared `movRegReg` emits. Same 64 bits, other file.
			bitcastF64ToI64 then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64FmovGprFp(dest, src: src))
			// Its INVERSE — `FMOV Dd, Xn`, the same general↔vector form read the other way, and again
			// NOT the `FMOV Dd, Dn` a plain FP copy would use.
			bitcastI64ToF64 then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64FmovFpGpr(dest, src: src))
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerUnaryOp'

	// Lower a `binOpImm` (constant right operand) to its arm64 immediate form. arm64's immediate ALU
	// ops are 3-address (dest independent of src), so plain defs — unlike x64's two-address imm forms.
	//
	// ⚠ AN IMMEDIATE OPERAND IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY AN IMMEDIATE FORM HERE. `foldConstOperands` is
	// TARGET-NEUTRAL and its per-opcode rule is sized for x64, where every direct-immediate form carries
	// a verbatim signed 32-bit field; AArch64's fields are narrower and stranger — 24 bits for add/sub,
	// rotated bitmasks only for the logicals, and none at all for `mul`. So `add`/`sub` and the logicals
	// ASK their encoder's own predicate before selecting the `#imm` form, `mul` never has one to select,
	// and all three fall back the same way: materialise the constant into the IP scratch, use the
	// register form.
	function lowerBinOpImm(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, imm Imm32, opcode StdBinOpcode, dstRef BlockRef)
		// EC16 — an element-index scaling the shifted-register `ADD` above carries. Neither the scratch
		// materialisation nor the `MUL` is emitted.
		if self.scaledIndexFolds.retiresScaleDef(resultId) 'foldedIntoAddress'
			return
		end 'foldedIntoAddress'

		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let src = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.lowerAddSubImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm, isSub: false, dstRef: dstRef)
			sub then self.lowerAddSubImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm, isSub: true, dstRef: dstRef)
			mul then self.lowerMulImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm, dstRef: dstRef)
			bitAnd to bitXor then self.lowerLogicalImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm, opcode: opcode, dstRef: dstRef)
			shl to shrLogical then self.lowerShiftImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm, instr: arm64ShiftInstrOf(opcode), dstRef: dstRef)
			div to max then panic("lowerBinOpImm: `{opcode.name}` is FLOAT-ONLY and no NEON form takes an immediate — foldConstOperands must never produce this")
			mulHighSigned then panic("lowerBinOpImm: `mulHighSigned` has no immediate form on any target — `constFitsBinOpImmediate` refuses every constant for it, so foldConstOperands must never produce this")
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerBinOpImm'

	// Materialise `imm` into the IP scratch and hand back the register naming it — the step every "no
	// immediate form fits" fallback in this file shares. The materialisation and the op that reads it
	// are emitted adjacently; see `Arm64Scratch` for why that is NOT the invariant the scheme rests on
	// (the splitter splices between them, measured) and for what actually is.
	function materialiseIntoScratch(imm Imm32, dstRef BlockRef) returns TargetVReg
		let scratch = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(scratch, value: imm32ToWord(imm)))
		return scratch
	end 'materialiseIntoScratch'

	// `dest = src + imm` / `dest = src - imm`. The AArch64 ADD/SUB-immediate form is a 12-bit field with
	// an optional left-shift by 12, so it reaches 24 bits' worth of value and NOTHING between there and
	// the `Imm32` ceiling the shared fold hands us. A constant past that reach has no immediate form at
	// all and takes the register one instead. `arm64ImmIsEncodableAddSub` is the ENCODER's own ceiling,
	// so this choice can never disagree with what it will encode.
	//
	// ⚠ THE BAND IS DECIDED BY THE MAGNITUDE, NOT THE VALUE: a negative literal on the ADD side becomes
	// a SUB of its magnitude (and vice versa), so `x + -1000000007` is as inencodable as
	// `x - 1000000007`. The materialised constant is the immediate's full 64-bit two's complement, which
	// is what keeps the register form's answer identical to the immediate form's on both signs.
	function lowerAddSubImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32, isSub bool, dstRef BlockRef)
		if arm64ImmIsEncodableAddSub(imm) 'fits'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64SubImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm) if isSub else TargetOp.arm64AddImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm))
			return
		end 'fits'

		let scratch = self.materialiseIntoScratch(imm, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Sub(dest, lhs: src, rhs: scratch) if isSub else TargetOp.arm64Add(dest, lhs: src, rhs: scratch))
	end 'lowerAddSubImm'

	// `dest = src * imm`. AArch64 has no `mul` immediate form, so the fallback is a `movz`/`movk` ladder
	// into the IP scratch plus a register `MUL` — TWO instructions and a scratch clobber for what x64
	// does in one `imul r64, r/m64, imm32`. `arm64MulImmForm` names the two shapes that escape it, and
	// carries the reason the ADMISSION rule upstream is deliberately not narrowed to match.
	function lowerMulImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64MulImmForm(imm, scale: self.mulScale) 'form'
			shiftLeft then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LslImm(dest, src: src, imm: arm64MulImmShiftCount(imm)))
			// `src * (2^k + 1)` is `src + (src << k)` — both operands of the shifted-register add name
			// the SAME register, which is what makes one address op a multiply.
			addShifted then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AddLsl(dest, base: src, index: src, scale: self.mulScale.value))
			registerMultiply then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Mul(dest, lhs: src, rhs: self.materialiseIntoScratch(imm, dstRef: dstRef)))
		end 'form'
	end 'lowerMulImm'

	// `dest = src <op> imm` for a logical op (`and`/`or`/`xor`). AArch64 `#bimm` forms encode ONLY
	// rotated-bitmask patterns, so a value that is not one (e.g. `10` = 0b1010) cannot use the immediate
	// form — the ENCODER would refuse it. `arm64ImmIsEncodableLogical` is the encoder's own predicate,
	// so this choice can never disagree with what it will encode. A non-bitmask immediate is materialised
	// into the IP scratch (x16, exactly as `lowerAddSubImm` and `lowerMulImm`) and the register form is
	// used.
	function lowerLogicalImm(dest TargetVReg, src TargetVReg, imm Imm32, opcode StdBinOpcode, dstRef BlockRef)
		if arm64ImmIsEncodableLogical(imm) 'bitmask'
			match opcode 'imm'
				bitAnd then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AndImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm))
				bitOr then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64OrrImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm))
				bitXor then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64EorImm(dest, src: src, imm: imm))
				default panic("lowerLogicalImm: non-logical opcode {opcode.rawValue}")
			end 'imm'
			return
		end 'bitmask'

		let scratch = self.materialiseIntoScratch(imm, dstRef: dstRef)
		match opcode 'reg'
			bitAnd then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64And(dest, lhs: src, rhs: scratch))
			bitOr then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Orr(dest, lhs: src, rhs: scratch))
			bitXor then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Eor(dest, lhs: src, rhs: scratch))
			default panic("lowerLogicalImm: non-logical opcode {opcode.rawValue}")
		end 'reg'
	end 'lowerLogicalImm'

	// The `[base + disp]` operand a data access finally addresses through — the selector's answer to
	// "does this offset have a direct addressing form here, and if not, what does".
	//
	// ⚠ AArch64's scaled load/store field is a 12-bit count of ACCESS UNITS, so its reach depends on the
	// WIDTH: 4095 bytes for an `LDRB`/`STRB`, 32760 for the 64-bit forms — and NOTHING between there and
	// the `Imm32` `memoryDisplacement` hands us, which is sized for x64's `[base + disp32]`. A field
	// occupies `Project.fieldSlotCount` 8-byte slots — ONE for every declared type but an INTERFACE one,
	// which takes two (the 16-byte fat pointer) — so those ceilings fall at the 512th and the 4096th SLOT,
	// which is the 512th/4096th ordinary field or the 256th/2048th existential one. A program that
	// declares that many is a program a generator writes.
	//
	// When no direct form exists the address is folded into the IP scratch and the access addresses
	// `[scratch + 0]`. The fold goes through `lowerAddSubImm` rather than a second copy of the same
	// choice: an offset inside the add/sub band becomes one `add x16, base, #imm` (or the hi/lo pair),
	// and a wider one the `movz`/`movk` ladder that arm already owns — so the folded form's reach IS the
	// arithmetic band, stated once, and cannot come to disagree with it.
	function addressFor(base TargetVReg, offset ByteOffset, width X64AccessWidth, dstRef BlockRef) returns Arm64MemOperand
		let disp = memoryDisplacement(offset)
		if arm64DispHasScaledForm(disp, width: width) 'direct'
			return Arm64MemOperand.create(base, disp: disp)
		end 'direct'

		let scratch = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
		self.lowerAddSubImm(scratch, src: base, imm: disp, isSub: false, dstRef: dstRef)
		return Arm64MemOperand.create(scratch, disp: 0)
	end 'addressFor'

	function lowerLoadIndirect(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		let width = accessWidthFor(valueType)
		// EC16 — the address is a `base + index*scale` chain nothing else reads, and it is one the
		// register-offset form can hold, so the access carries the whole address and the `add` that
		// computed it emitted nothing.
		if self.scaledIndexFolds.fusesIntoMemoryOp(addrId) 'indexed'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseIndexScale(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), base: TargetVReg.virtual(self.scaledIndexFolds.baseOf(addrId)), index: TargetVReg.virtual(self.scaledIndexFolds.indexOf(addrId)), scale: self.scaledIndexFolds.scaleOf(addrId), disp: memoryDisplacement(offset), width: width))
			return
		end 'indexed'

		let mem = self.addressFor(TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), offset: offset, width: width, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), base: mem.base, disp: mem.disp, width: width))
	end 'lowerLoadIndirect'

	function lowerStoreIndirect(addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueId ValueId, valueType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		let width = accessWidthFor(valueType)
		// EC16 — the store mirror of the indexed load above.
		if self.scaledIndexFolds.fusesIntoMemoryOp(addrId) 'indexed'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.storeBaseIndexScaleReg(TargetVReg.virtual(self.scaledIndexFolds.baseOf(addrId)), index: TargetVReg.virtual(self.scaledIndexFolds.indexOf(addrId)), scale: self.scaledIndexFolds.scaleOf(addrId), disp: memoryDisplacement(offset), src: TargetVReg.virtual(valueId), width: width))
			return
		end 'indexed'

		let mem = self.addressFor(TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), offset: offset, width: width, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(mem.base, disp: mem.disp, src: TargetVReg.virtual(valueId), width: width))
	end 'lowerStoreIndirect'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **G3 — THE ADDRESS AN ORDERED ACCESS NEEDS: A BARE BASE REGISTER, WITH THE FIELD OFFSET ALREADY
	// FOLDED IN.** `LDAR`/`STLR` are load/store-EXCLUSIVE-group encodings, whose only addressing form is
	// `[Xn]` — there is no displacement field to put `offset` in. So an ordered access at a non-zero field
	// offset costs one extra `add` into the IP scratch, and at offset 0 costs nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS `addressFor`'s FOLD AND NOT A SECOND COPY OF IT.** That function already answers "this
	// displacement has no direct form here — put `base + disp` in x16", through `lowerAddSubImm`, so the
	// reach of the fold (the add/sub immediate band, then the `movz`/`movk` ladder) is stated once. Asking
	// it for a width whose scaled form CANNOT hold the displacement would be a lie about why the fold
	// happened; asking `arm64DispHasScaledForm` here would be that predicate written twice. What this does
	// instead is state the ordered forms' OWN rule — zero offset or fold — which is a different rule from
	// `addressFor`'s and therefore its own two lines.
	//
	// ⚠ **x16 IS SAFE ACROSS THE PAIR FOR `addressFor`'s REASON, WHICH IS THE STANDING ONE**
	// (`Arm64Scratch`'s header): nothing a post-isel pass splices writes x16 — a spliced wide-frame address
	// fold writes x17 — so no reload can land between the `add` and the access that reads it.
	function orderedAccessBase(addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, dstRef BlockRef) returns TargetVReg
		let base = TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)
		let disp = memoryDisplacement(offset)
		if disp == 0 'alreadyTheAddress'
			return base
		end 'alreadyTheAddress'

		let scratch = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
		self.lowerAddSubImm(scratch, src: base, imm: disp, isSub: false, dstRef: dstRef)
		return scratch
	end 'orderedAccessBase'

	function lowerLoadAcquire(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LoadAcquire(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), base: self.orderedAccessBase(addrId, offset: offset, dstRef: dstRef), width: accessWidthFor(valueType)))
	end 'lowerLoadAcquire'

	function lowerStoreRelease(addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueId ValueId, valueType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64StoreRelease(self.orderedAccessBase(addrId, offset: offset, dstRef: dstRef), src: TargetVReg.virtual(valueId), width: accessWidthFor(valueType)))
	end 'lowerStoreRelease'

	// Lower a Std `div`/`mod` to `sdiv`/`udiv` (+ `msub` for the remainder). NO zero-divisor guard: AArch64
	// `sdiv`/`udiv` return 0 on a zero divisor (defined behaviour, unlike x64's `idiv` #DE), and the spec
	// adopts that — `a / 0` is 0, and the divide/modulo-by-zero tests are gated to x64-windows (see the
	// file header / safety.md). So the division lowers straight into `dstRef` and never splits the block.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SIGNEDNESS IS THE OP'S, NOT AN OPERAND'S** (X10, `IntegerSignedness`). arm64 was the target
	// where the SIGNED reading happened to be harmless for the sibling operators — a "32-bit" binop is
	// emitted at 64 bits here — but a divide's signedness is real on every target, and `udiv` was already
	// declared, printed, coloured and encoded with only this isel never reaching for it.
	//
	// `mod` is `x − (x/y)*y` via the divide then `msub`, and the SAME `msub` serves both signednesses:
	// the subtraction and the multiply are two's-complement identical, so only the quotient's instruction
	// differs. The quotient is an intermediate the isel cannot mint a fresh virtual for (the parser owns
	// the value space), so it goes through the IP scratch x16: `sdiv x16, lhs, divisor; msub dest, x16,
	// divisor, lhs`. x16 is non-allocatable, so no value is coloured into it across the pair — see
	// `Arm64Scratch` for why "adjacent" is not the argument and what is.
	function lowerDivMod(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, isRemainder bool, signedness IntegerSignedness, dstRef BlockRef)
		let divisor = TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)
		let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(resultId)
		let lhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		if isRemainder 'mod'
			let quot = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
			self.emit(dstRef, op: arm64DivideOp(quot, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: divisor, signedness: signedness))
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Msub(dest, mulLhs: quot, mulRhs: divisor, minuend: lhsReg))
		end 'mod' else 'div'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: arm64DivideOp(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: divisor, signedness: signedness))
		end 'div'
	end 'lowerDivMod'

	// Lower a Std `cmp` to `cmp`/`fcmp`, plus a `cset` when something reads the boolean out of a REGISTER.
	// Two INDEPENDENT consumers, exactly as x64 has: the branch terminating this block may read the flags
	// (`lowerCondBranch`), and anything holding the compare as a value needs the `cset`. Both at once is
	// legal — `cset` reads NZCV and writes none, so the `b.cond` still sees the compare's flags.
	function lowerCmp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		let isFloat = stdTypeIsFloat(operandType)
		if isFloat 'float'
			arm64RequireScalarDouble(operandType, what: "lowerCmp")
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Fcmp(TargetVReg.virtual(lhs), rhs: TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)))
		end 'float' else 'int'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Cmp(TargetVReg.virtual(lhs), rhs: TargetVReg.virtual(rhs)))
		end 'int'
		self.materialiseCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerCmp'

	// Write a compare's boolean into its register — but only when something actually reads it as a value.
	// A compare read ONLY by the branch it fuses with needs no register at all, which is why `if a > b`
	// lowers to a bare `cmp` + `b.gt`.
	//
	// ⭐ THE CONDITION IS `arm64CondForPred`'s, THE SAME MAPPING THE FUSED BRANCH ASKS FOR, and that is
	// what keeps the two spellings of one predicate from drifting: `cset cond` + `cbnz` branches when
	// `cond` holds, and `b.cond` branches when `cond` holds. Neither ever inverts, so the NaN-safe float
	// conditions (`mi`/`ls`/`gt`/`ge`) are preserved and their TRUE-on-NaN complements `pl`/`hi` stay
	// unreachable — see `Arm64CondCode`.
	function materialiseCompareIfRead(resultId ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		if not self.compareFusion.valueIsReadFromRegister(resultId) 'flagsOnly'
			return
		end 'flagsOnly'
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Cset(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), cond: arm64CondForPred(pred, isFloat: stdTypeIsFloat(operandType), isUnsigned: stdTypeIsUnsigned(operandType))))
	end 'materialiseCompareIfRead'

	// Lower a Std `cmpImm` (constant right operand) to `cmp reg, #imm` + `cset`. `cmpImm` is INTEGER by
	// construction (no NEON form takes an immediate, so `foldConstOperands` refuses a float rhs) — which
	// is why the condition code is ASKED of the op's own `operandType` (X5 review) rather than told
	// `isFloat: false`. The derived answer is that same `false`, by that same guard; what changes is that
	// it can no longer be a stale copy of it. `lowerCmp` next door already reads its `operandType` this
	// way, and the pair now spells the question once.
	//
	// ⚠ AND AN IMMEDIATE OPERAND IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY AN IMMEDIATE FORM, for `lowerBinOpImm`'s reason at
	// a NARROWER band. A compare-immediate is one imm12 field, plain or shifted by 12 — it cannot use the
	// hi/lo PAIR `+`/`-` split into, because the flags it exists to set would describe only the second
	// half of the subtraction. So `x + 16777215` has an immediate form and `x == 16777215` does not, and
	// the selector asks `arm64ImmIsEncodableCmp` — the ENCODER's own band — before selecting one.
	//
	// The fallback is the materialisation every other "no immediate form fits" arm in this file uses. It
	// used to happen inside `arm64EmitCmpImm`, i.e. AFTER register allocation, where a write of x16 is
	// invisible to the allocator, to the liveness model and to the emitted IR — safe only because nothing
	// happened to place such an op between an ISel-minted `movz`/consumer pair, which nothing checked.
	function lowerCmpImm(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, imm Imm32, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstRef BlockRef)
		let lhsReg = TargetVReg.virtual(lhs)
		if arm64ImmIsEncodableCmp(imm) 'fits'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64CmpImm(lhsReg, imm: imm))
		end 'fits' else 'materialise'
			let scratch = self.materialiseIntoScratch(imm, dstRef: dstRef)
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Cmp(lhsReg, rhs: scratch))
		end 'materialise'

		self.materialiseCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerCmpImm'

	// Lower a Std `condBranch` to a conditional branch to `<then>` plus a `b <else>`. The conditional is a
	// body op and the `b` is the block's terminator; the unconditional edge is emitted for EVERY layout so
	// correctness never rests on block ordering (a later pass may elide a fall-through).
	//
	// TWO shapes, and which one applies is settled BEFORE the walk by the fusion census, never guessed:
	//
	//   • FUSED — the compare defining `condId` is the last flag-clobbering op of this block, so the NZCV
	//     it set are exactly the ones the branch reads. `b.cond` tests that compare's predicate directly
	//     and no `cset` was emitted.
	//   • NOT FUSED — the condition is an ordinary boolean VALUE in a register: a `bool` param, a call's
	//     `bool` return, a short-circuit merge phi, a `let`-bound compare read a few statements later, or
	//     a compare whose own block's branch could not fuse. `cbnz` is the register zero-test arm64 has
	//     and x64 does not, so no `cmp reg, 0` is needed.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **`b.cond` READS NZCV WHERE `cbnz` READS A REGISTER, SO THE FUSED SHAPE HAS A FLAG LIFETIME AND
	// THE ZERO-TEST SHAPE HAS NONE.** Any instruction between the compare and the branch that writes NZCV
	// destroys the fusion. arm64 needs no rule of its own beyond the census's, and that rests on ONE
	// property of this file: **every arm64 lowering that writes NZCV sits on a Std op declaring
	// `clobbersFlags: true`** — `cmp`/`fcmp` here, the `cmp` inside `arm64MemFill`'s size ladder, the
	// CAS/RMW retry loops, and every call. The one lowering that writes flags for a reason of its own is
	// `lowerTlsSlotLoad`'s uninitialised-offset gate, and `StdOp.tlsSlotLoad` declares the clobber for it.
	// ⇒ **A NEW arm64 LOWERING THAT EMITS AN S-SUFFIXED FORM (`adds`/`subs`/`ands`), A `cmp`/`cmn` OR AN
	// `fcmp` MUST CHECK ITS STD OP'S DECLARATION**, or a `b.cond` somewhere will read its flags instead of
	// the compare's — silently, and only in the block that happens to contain both.
	//
	// The non-S forms this file leans on make the rest free: `add`/`sub`/`orr`/`eor`/`and`, the shifts,
	// `madd`/`msub`, `sdiv`/`udiv`, `adrp`+`add`, `movz`/`movk` and every load/store write no flag at all.
	function lowerCondBranch(condId ValueId, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, dstRef BlockRef)
		if not self.compareFusion.blockFusesCompare(dstRef) 'notFused'
			self.emitCondBranch(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Cbnz(TargetVReg.virtual(condId), targetBlockId: thenBlockId))
			self.emitTerminator(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Branch(elseBlockId))
			return
		end 'notFused'

		let cond = arm64CondForPred(self.compareFusion.fusedPredicateOf(dstRef), isFloat: self.compareFusion.fusedCompareIsFloat(condId), isUnsigned: self.compareFusion.fusedCompareIsUnsigned(condId))
		self.emitCondBranch(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64CondBranch(cond, targetBlockId: thenBlockId))
		self.emitTerminator(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Branch(elseBlockId))
	end 'lowerCondBranch'

	// Value return: move the value into the primary-return register (x0 for an integer, d0 for a float),
	// then `ret`. Frame teardown is the prologue/epilogue pass's job.
	function lowerReturn(retValId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		self.emitPrimaryReturnMove(retValId, dstRef: dstRef, returnType: returnType)
		self.emitRet(dstRef, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerReturn'

	// Void return: nothing moves to the return register. The declared type must AGREE — a `retVoid` in a
	// value-returning function would leave x0 unwritten, handing the caller garbage; the parser rejects
	// that source (E3013), so a disagreement here is a lowering bug.
	function lowerVoidReturn(dstRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		if not stdReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'valueExpected'
			panic("lowerStdToArm64: a `retVoid` in a value-returning function — the return register would never be written and the caller would read garbage")
		end 'valueExpected'
		self.emitRet(dstRef, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerVoidReturn'

	function emitRet(dstRef BlockRef, asTerminator bool)
		if asTerminator 'asTerm'
			self.emitTerminator(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Ret)
		end 'asTerm' else 'asBody'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Ret)
		end 'asBody'
	end 'emitRet'

	// Move a return VALUE into its AAPCS64 register: x0 for an integer, d0 for a float. Shared by the
	// ordinary `ret` (via `emitPrimaryReturnMove`, which rejects a void return type first) and the throwing
	// `errorReturn` (whose void form legitimately carries a family-default zero-int value:
	// `stdReturnTypeIsFloat(void)` is `false`, so it lands in x0, where a GPR zero belongs).
	function emitReturnValueMove(retValId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType)
		let retReg = Arm64FloatReturnReg if stdReturnTypeIsFloat(returnType) else Arm64IntReturnReg
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(retReg), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(retValId)))
	end 'emitReturnValueMove'

	// The ordinary `ret`'s primary move. A `void` reaching here is a value `ret` (`StdOp.ret`) inside a void
	// function — a lowering bug, since a void return lowers to `retVoid` (guarded oppositely in
	// `lowerVoidReturn`). The throwing `errorReturn` path does NOT come through here — its void form has a
	// real dummy value to place, so it calls `emitReturnValueMove` directly.
	function emitPrimaryReturnMove(retValId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType)
		if stdReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'void'
			panic("lowerStdToArm64: a value `ret` in a void-returning function — a void return lowers to `StdOp.retVoid`, which never reaches this path")
		end 'void'
		self.emitReturnValueMove(retValId, dstRef: dstRef, returnType: returnType)
	end 'emitPrimaryReturnMove'

	// Lower a Std `param` to the entry move that captures the parameter's incoming ABI register into its
	// virtual: `mov virtual(resultId), physical(argReg[slot])`. Modelled as a copy from a physical source
	// so the allocator's fixed-register hint biases the value toward its own argument register (the move
	// then self-elides when free).
	//
	// ⭐ A FLOAT parameter captures from a SIMD arg register (d0..d7) chosen by a SEPARATE FP counter —
	// the slot is the count of preceding SAME-FILE parameters (`abiFileSlotIndex`, the cross-target
	// ABI contract), NOT the source `index`. The parameter value is XMM-classed by the value-class column
	// (from its f64 Std type), so `movRegReg` sees two FP ends (`fmov`); an integer parameter captures from
	// x0..x7 exactly as before.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ A STACK PARAMETER IS LOADED, NOT CAPTURED, exactly as on x64 — but arm64 cannot yet say from
	// WHERE. Its incoming arguments sit at `[x29 + frameSize - outgoingArgSize + slot*8]`, and neither term
	// is known until the allocator has finished. So the isel names the SLOT and `Arm64PrologueEpilogue` —
	// the one pass that knows both — resolves `arm64LoadIncomingArg` into a concrete `[x29 + disp]` load.
	function lowerParam(resultId ValueId, index ParamCount, paramType StdType, paramFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstRef BlockRef)
		let capacity = arm64RegisterArgCapacity()
		if abiArgIsOnStack(paramFloatMask, index: index, capacity: capacity) 'stackParam'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LoadIncomingArg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), slot: abiStackSlotsBefore(paramFloatMask, index: index, capacity: capacity) as SpillSlot))
			return
		end 'stackParam'

		let slot = abiFileSlotIndex(paramFloatMask, index: index)
		let argReg = arm64FpArgRegisterAt(slot) if stdTypeIsFloat(paramType) else arm64ArgRegisterAt(slot)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(argReg)))
	end 'lowerParam'

	// The physical register a call to `callee` returns its PRIMARY value in — **d0** for a float-returning
	// callee, **x0** otherwise. Read off the SAME checked name index the value-class column uses
	// (`calleeResultClass`), so the captured value is coloured in the file the register it reads from lives
	// in: a float result is XMM-classed, so `movRegReg` sees two FP ends (`fmov`), never a cross-file move.
	function callResultRegister(callee ByteArray) returns Arm64Register
		return Arm64FloatReturnReg if calleeResultClass(self.funcNames, callee: callee) == RegClass.xmm else Arm64IntReturnReg
	end 'callResultRegister'

	// The DIRECT-call argument moves: each argument's register FILE is the CALLEE's declared PARAM class,
	// looked up by name and collected into an `ArgFloatMask` (`calleeArgFloatMask`), then slotted and emitted
	// through the shared `emitArgMovesByFloatMask`. Reading the callee's own parameter files is what makes a
	// float written to d0 here read back from d0 in the callee — the mask this hands the placement rule is
	// the very one the callee's `lowerParam` folds out of its declared parameter types. Shared by `lowerCall`
	// and `lowerTryCall`, whose argument passing is identical.
	function emitCallArgMoves(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args, argFloatMask: calleeArgFloatMask(self.calleeParamClasses, callee: callee, argCount: args.count(), registerCapacity: arm64RegisterArgCapacity()), dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'emitCallArgMoves'

	// Lower a Std `call`: move each argument into its ABI register (`emitCallArgMoves`), `bl` the callee,
	// then move the return value (`callResultRegister`) into the result.
	function lowerCall(resultId ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitCallArgMoves(callee, args: args, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(callee))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(self.callResultRegister(callee))))
	end 'lowerCall'

	// Lower a Std `callIndirect` (P1.5-A1): move each argument into its ABI register, `blr` THROUGH the
	// callee register (`arm64BranchLinkReg` carries `calleeReg` as an explicit USE, so the allocator keeps
	// the callee address live to the call), then capture the return value into the result. The mirror of
	// `lowerCall`: there is no callee NAME to class the result or arguments against, so both come from the
	// function value's SIGNATURE carried on the op (P1.5 #78) — a float result is captured from **d0** (else
	// **x0**), and each argument is routed by its own type (a float to d0..d7, else x0..x7).
	function lowerCallIndirect(resultId ValueId, calleeReg ValueId, args ValueIdArray, resultType StdReturnType, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLinkReg(TargetVReg.virtual(calleeReg)))
		self.captureIndirectResult(resultId, resultType: resultType, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerCallIndirect'

	// Capture the primary result of a call made through a POINTER — an indirect call or either witness form.
	// A VALUE callee's result comes from d0/x0 into `resultId`. A VOID callee (a raw element destructor, a
	// void interface method) leaves nothing to capture — `resultId` is a dead slot with no def, so no move is
	// emitted and it never enters liveness/coloring. Shared by the three so they cannot drift on the result
	// register, exactly as `emitArgMovesByFloatMask` keeps them from drifting on argument slotting.
	function captureIndirectResult(resultId ValueId, resultType StdReturnType, dstRef BlockRef)
		match resultType 'r'
			void then break 'r'
			value(inner) then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(arm64IndirectResultRegister(inner))))
		end 'r'
	end 'captureIndirectResult'

	// ⭐ **LOWER THE FUSED `witnessCall` (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-C).** arm64 has no memory-operand call, so where
	// x64 emits one `call qword [witnessAddr + 24 + 8*i]` (`callMem`), arm64 spells the same thing in two:
	// `ldr x16, [witnessAddr, #slotOffset]` then `blr x16`. This is the SAME shape the GOT-indirect import
	// call already uses (`arm64EmitImportCall`: `adrp x16` / `ldr x16, [x16]` / `blr x16`), so the idiom is
	// the backend's established one rather than a new invention.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LOAD IS EMITTED AFTER THE ARGUMENT MOVES, AND THE ORDER IS A CORRECTNESS REQUIREMENT.** x16 is
	// the IP scratch: it is outside the allocatable pool precisely so the ENCODER may clobber it at will (a
	// large `movImm`, an out-of-reach displacement, a spill/reload address). Loading the callee before the
	// argument setup would leave it live across every one of those, and the clobber would be a jump to a
	// garbage address with no diagnostic. Emitting it between the last argument move and the `blr` leaves it
	// live across exactly ONE instruction boundary and nothing may be inserted there — the same discipline
	// `lowerDivMod` states for its `sdiv`/`msub` quotient.
	//
	// `witnessAddr` — a normal virtual — is what stays live across the argument moves instead, which is safe
	// for the reason x64's twin gives about `callMem`'s base register: each argument move is a PHYSICAL def,
	// and liveness adds every such def to `forbiddenPhys` for the values live across it, so the table address
	// can never be coloured onto an argument register the moves have already written.
	function lowerWitnessCall(resultId ValueId, witnessAddr ValueId, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, args ValueIdArray, resultType StdReturnType, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitWitnessCallSequence(witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.captureIndirectResult(resultId, resultType: resultType, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerWitnessCall'

	// The argument marshalling, the slot load and the `blr` — everything a witness call does BEFORE its
	// results are captured. ONE home, shared by the throwing and non-throwing forms so the two cannot drift
	// on the slot offset or on argument slotting; a divergence there would be a silently mis-dispatched
	// method, not a compile error.
	function emitWitnessCallSequence(witnessAddr ValueId, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, args ValueIdArray, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
		let callee = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
		// Through `addressFor` like every other data access, so the slot offset's addressability is ASKED
		// rather than assumed: an interface past 4092 methods puts its later slots outside the scaled field,
		// and a second un-asked spelling here is exactly how a wrong-field read gets written down twice. The
		// fold's scratch is x16 — the register the callee is about to land in — which is why it is safe: the
		// `add` and the `ldr` that consumes it stay adjacent, and the load overwrites its own address.
		let slot = self.addressFor(TargetVReg.virtual(witnessAddr), offset: witnessMethodSlotOffset(methodIndex), width: X64AccessWidth.word64, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(callee, base: slot.base, disp: slot.disp, width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLinkReg(callee))
	end 'emitWitnessCallSequence'

	// ⭐ **LOWER THE THROWING FUSED `witnessTryCall` (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-C)** — `lowerWitnessCall` plus the one
	// thing `lowerTryCall` adds to `lowerCall`: the error flag captured from **x9** (`Arm64FlagReturnReg`).
	// The two capture moves are a PARALLEL COPY and are resequenced by the same target-agnostic machinery
	// `lowerTryCall`'s pair is (`colorAndDropSelfMoves`), which keys on a call op followed by two
	// virtual-dest/physical-src moves — `arm64BranchLinkReg` is a call, so the fused witness call satisfies
	// that test as `arm64BranchLink` does, and a result/flag colouring cycle is broken rather than collapsed.
	//
	// A VOID throwing method captures nothing for its primary, leaving the flag capture alone after the call —
	// a single move, with no cycle to break, which is correct: one move cannot overwrite its own source.
	function lowerWitnessTryCall(resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId, witnessAddr ValueId, methodIndex WitnessMethodIndex, args ValueIdArray, resultType StdReturnType, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitWitnessCallSequence(witnessAddr, methodIndex: methodIndex, args: args, argFloatMask: argFloatMask, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.captureIndirectResult(resultId, resultType: resultType, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(errorFlagId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64FlagReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerWitnessTryCall'

	// Place each argument given a per-argument float mask (bit k set iff argument k is a float) — the arm64
	// twin of x64's `emitArgMovesByFloatMask` — into its file's ABI register (d0..d7 for a float, x0..x7
	// otherwise) while that file has one, else into this frame's outgoing STACK region at `[SP + slot*8]`,
	// the AAPCS64 layout. Every placement decision comes from the shared rule, the SAME calls the callee's
	// `lowerParam` makes; this routine used to run its own gpr/fp counters, a second spelling of it.
	// The ONE slotting+emit path a DIRECT call (`emitCallArgMoves`, mask from the callee's declared param
	// files), an INDIRECT call (`lowerCallIndirect`) and a witness call all route through.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **EVERY STACK STORE IS EMITTED BEFORE EVERY REGISTER MOVE**, for the reason x64's twin states in
	// full and which the corpus proved there: materializing a stack argument's SOURCE takes a register the
	// allocator may pick, and nothing marks an already-established ARGUMENT register live from its move to
	// the call, so a store emitted after one can silently overwrite it. Both reference compilers order it
	// this way on both of their targets. Once no argument register has been established while the stores
	// run, the hazard is gone rather than narrowed.
	//
	// Forward-order emission within the register phase is then sound (each physical arg-register def
	// liveness forbids to values live across it, keyed on `RegNum` and class-agnostic), now covering the
	// SIMD arg registers too.
	function emitArgMovesByFloatMask(args ValueIdArray, argFloatMask ArgFloatMask, dstRef BlockRef)
		let capacity = arm64RegisterArgCapacity()
		for k in 0 upto args.count() 'eachStackArg'
			if not abiArgIsOnStack(argFloatMask, index: k, capacity: capacity) 'inRegister'
				continue
			end 'inRegister'
			let argId = try args.get(k) otherwise panic("emitArgMovesByFloatMask: args.get OOB at {k}")
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64StoreOutgoingArg(abiStackSlotsBefore(argFloatMask, index: k, capacity: capacity) as SpillSlot, srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(argId)))
		end 'eachStackArg'

		for k in 0 upto args.count() 'eachRegisterArg'
			if abiArgIsOnStack(argFloatMask, index: k, capacity: capacity) 'onStack'
				continue
			end 'onStack'
			let argId = try args.get(k) otherwise panic("emitArgMovesByFloatMask: args.get OOB at {k}")
			let slot = abiFileSlotIndex(argFloatMask, index: k)
			if argMaskBit(argFloatMask, argIndex: k) 'floatArg'
				self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(arm64FpArgRegisterAt(slot)), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(argId)))
			end 'floatArg' else 'intArg'
				self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(arm64ArgRegisterAt(slot)), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(argId)))
			end 'intArg'
		end 'eachRegisterArg'
	end 'emitArgMovesByFloatMask'

	// The arm64 twin of x64's `lowerTryCall` (P1.4b): args + `bl` as `lowerCall`, then capture BOTH return
	// registers — the primary value from `callResultRegister(callee)` (**d0** for a float-returning callee,
	// else **x0**) and the SECOND WORD from **x9**. That word is the error flag for a THROWING callee and the
	// WITNESS half for an interface-returning one (P1.7a slice 2); the ABI is the same either way, and
	// `functionUsesSecondaryReturnSlot` is the one predicate that decides a callee writes it. The two capture
	// moves are resequenced through the
	// parallel-copy sequencer (`colorAndDropSelfMoves`), exactly as x64's are — the resequencer is
	// target-agnostic (it reads whatever physical sources the captures name). Emitted consecutively right
	// after the call so its "call followed by two virtual-dest/physical-src moves" test identifies the pair.
	// A float primary (d0) and the GPR flag (x9) are in different files, so the pair has no cross-file cycle.
	function lowerTryCall(resultId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitCallArgMoves(callee, args: args, dstRef: dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(callee))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(self.callResultRegister(callee))))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(errorFlagId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64FlagReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerTryCall'

	// The arm64 dual-register `errorReturn`: write the value to its return register (**d0** for a float
	// return, else **x0** — through the SAME `emitReturnValueMove` the ordinary `ret` uses) and the SECOND
	// WORD to **x9** — the error flag for a THROWING function, the WITNESS half for an interface-returning one
	// (P1.7a slice 2), a difference this emitter cannot see and does not need to — then the return terminator (which `Arm64PrologueEpilogue` recognizes exactly as an ordinary
	// return's, since this emits the same `arm64Ret`). Both moves unconditional, mirroring x64's
	// `lowerErrorReturn`; a void return type targets x0, where the family-default zero belongs.
	//
	// A THROWING FLOAT function's THROW path IS handled (F3b / OPEN #60, same fix as x64's `lowerErrorReturn`):
	// `lowerThrowError` (LowerMaxonToStd) types the throw-edge primary default to the return type via
	// `emitZeroConstOfReturnType` — a d0-classed float zero for a float return — so both the OK path (real
	// float → d0) and the throw path (a dead d0 zero the caller ignores) reach `emitReturnValueMove` correctly classed.
	function lowerErrorReturn(retValId ValueId, errorFlagId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		self.emitReturnValueMove(retValId, dstRef: dstRef, returnType: returnType)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64FlagReturnReg), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(errorFlagId)))
		self.emitRet(dstRef, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerErrorReturn'

	// ⭐ THE ONE DOOR EVERY OS PRIMITIVE GOES THROUGH. Emit the call that reaches the kernel: a
	// GOT-indirect libSystem call on macOS, a raw `svc #0` on Linux (`arm64OsCallKind` — see
	// Arm64Runtime for why the two OSes differ at all). Both take their arguments in x0.. and return
	// in x0, which is exactly why ONE door works: the three lowerings below emit IDENTICAL argument
	// moves and read the result from the same register on both OSes, and differ only in this call.
	//
	// Each caller names BOTH routes' identity for its primitive — the import slot and the syscall
	// number — so a primitive can never be wired to the right slot and the wrong number.
	function emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef BlockRef, importSlot OsImportSlot, syscallNumber MachineWord)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(importSlot)))
			syscall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Syscall(syscallNumber))
		end 'route'
	end 'emitOsPrimitiveCall'

	// Lower `osExit(code)` to the platform's process-exit primitive — libSystem `_exit` through the GOT
	// on macOS, `exit_group` via `svc #0` on Linux — the arm64 twin of x64's ExitProcess path (the same
	// primitive the arm64 entry stub uses). AAPCS64 and the Linux syscall ABI both put the code in x0.
	function lowerOsExit(codeId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(codeId)))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.exit, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallExitGroup)
	end 'lowerOsExit'

	// Lower `osPanic <label>` — a violated P1.9 range guard, or a user `panic("…")` — to `mrt_panic`,
	// which prints the baked `panic at …` line plus a symbolized backtrace and exits `PanicExitCode`. The
	// message address goes in x0 (AAPCS64's first argument) and the `bl` follows; `functionMakesCall`
	// sees the branch-with-link and frames the caller accordingly.
	//
	// ⭐ NO OS SPLIT HERE, exactly as x64's `lowerPanic` has none: x0 is not an OS fact, and the ONE
	// genuine per-OS difference — how the process finally exits — lives INSIDE the chunk, where
	// `Arm64PanicRuntime` derives it from the same `arm64OsCallKind` door this file consults for every
	// other primitive. This used to be a BARE EXIT, printing nothing on either arm64 lane, because
	// nobody had written an arm64 `mrt_panic`; `Arm64PanicRuntime` is that runtime.
	function lowerPanic(label ByteArray, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LeaRdata(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), label: label))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(PanicName))
	end 'lowerPanic'

	// The RENDERED twin (A3n). The message is a runtime POINTER — an interpolation assembled the whole
	// `panic at …\n` line, NUL terminator included — so it MOVES into x0 where the baked form materializes
	// a label address there. Everything after is identical: one `mrt_panic`, one cstr contract, two ways
	// of producing the bytes.
	function lowerPanicValue(messagePtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(messagePtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(PanicName))
	end 'lowerPanicValue'

	// Lower `osAllocPages(size)` to the POSIX page source `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
	// MAP_ANON|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)` — the arm64 twin of x64's `lowerOsAllocPages` (VirtualAlloc), and FAR
	// simpler: AAPCS64's argument registers x0..x7 ARE the call ABI, so there is no custom-ABI↔OS-ABI
	// mismatch to bridge (the whole reason x64 needs its own op rather than a `call`). The six arguments
	// go straight into x0..x5 and the result reads back from x0. MAP_ANON returns ZEROED pages, satisfying
	// the slab's zeroing contract (see the mmap constants), exactly as x64's MEM_COMMIT does.
	//
	// ⭐ THE ARGUMENT MOVES ARE OS-INVARIANT AND THE SYSCALL ABI IS WHY. Linux's `svc` reads its six
	// arguments from x0..x5 — the SAME registers AAPCS64 passes the libSystem call's in — so only the
	// call itself and the flag word change between the two OSes, not this sequence.
	//
	// Forward-order emission is sound for the one live operand (`size`): each `mov x[k], …` is a physical
	// def of x[k] that liveness forbids to every value live across it, so `size` (live into its own move)
	// can never be coloured to an argument register set before it — the same argument `lowerCall` makes.
	// The OS call's caller-saved clobber confines any value live ACROSS it to the callee-saved half,
	// which is what keeps `__slab_alloc`'s bookkeeping (live across the page call) correct — the exact
	// miscompile the x64 `iatCall` clobber comment records for `VirtualAlloc`.
	function lowerOsAllocPages(resultId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), value: MmapKernelChoosesBase))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: MmapProtReadWrite))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x3), value: mmapAnonPrivateFlags(self.target.os)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x4), value: MmapAnonFd))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x5), value: MmapNoOffset))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.mmap, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsAllocPages'

	// ⭐⭐ **`osCpuCount(result)` — ONE CALL PER LANE, AND THE TWO LANES DISAGREE ABOUT WHETHER IT NEEDS A
	// FRAME.** macOS asks `sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)`: one immediate into x0, one GOT call, the count out
	// of x0, and no chunk — the op has no operands and no out-param (`StdOp.osCpuCount` explains why it takes
	// no buffer), so nothing has to outlive the call. Linux has no `sysconf` to call at all (it is a libc
	// FUNCTION there, and this lane links no libc), so its answer is `sched_getaffinity` into a 128-byte
	// cpu-mask buffer and a POPCOUNT over it — a buffer and a loop, which is a chunk by the same structural
	// rule the host-surface lowerings below follow (`Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxCpuCountChunk`).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ANSWER IS LEFT EXACTLY AS THE OS GAVE IT, INCLUDING `sysconf`'s `-1`.** The `< 1 ⇒ 1` clamp is
	// builder-built Std's (`CpuParallelRuntime.buildCpuCount`), whose SIGNED test was written to cover both
	// this lane's `-1` and Windows's `0` — so clamping here as well would be one contract in two places, and
	// the x64 lowering deliberately does not clamp either. The Linux chunk answers `-1` for a failed
	// `sched_getaffinity` for exactly that reason: it reports the failure in the vocabulary the clamp already
	// reads rather than inventing a floor of its own.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT ASKS THE ROUTE ORACLE, AND WOULD HAVE TO EVEN IF BOTH ARMS WERE THE SAME SHAPE.** Every other
	// `arm64ImportCall` in this file is emitted through `emitOsPrimitiveCall`, so the `syscall` lanes
	// structurally cannot reach one; this primitive has no syscall route to give that door — its Linux arm is
	// a `bl`, not a `svc` — and emitting the import call unconditionally would put one on a lane with no
	// import table. That failure is NOT the loud kind the host-surface lowerings below get: `ElfWriter` never
	// reads the import relocations at all, so an unpatched `adrp x16, #0; ldr x16, [x16]; blr x16` would ship
	// as a jump through the first word of its own `.text` page, while a `bl <chunk>` into a chunk that lane
	// never built is a `CallFixup` and `arm64ResolveCallFixups` PANICS on it.
	function lowerOsCpuCount(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsCpuCountDarwin(dstRef)
			syscall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostCpuCountName))
		end 'route'

		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsCpuCount'

	// The libSystem arm: the `_SC_` parameter into x0 and the import. Its own function rather than an inline
	// arm because a `match` arm is one expression and this is two — `lowerOsFileOpenDarwin`'s split, for
	// `lowerOsFileOpenDarwin`'s reason.
	function lowerOsCpuCountDarwin(dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), value: DarwinScNprocessorsOnln))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.sysconf)))
	end 'lowerOsCpuCountDarwin'

	// ⭐⭐ **`osGetPid(result)` — THE SIMPLEST OS LOWERING IN THIS FILE, AND SINCE L3 IT NEEDS NO ROUTE ARM OF
	// ITS OWN.** It has no operands, no out-param and nothing that must outlive the call, so it is
	// `lowerOsExit`'s shape with a result — and both lanes now HAVE the primitive, libSystem's `getpid`
	// through the GOT and AArch64's syscall 172, which is exactly the pair `emitOsPrimitiveCall` exists to
	// choose between. Its band-mate `osExecutablePath` IS a chunk, because it must still hold the caller's
	// buffer pointer after a call that answers in a register — the split runs along that line and not along
	// which family an op is in.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ROUTE IS STILL ASKED, JUST NOT HERE.** `emitOsPrimitiveCall` asks it, which is strictly better
	// than the `match` this used to carry: a lowering that names BOTH routes' identity cannot be wired to the
	// right import slot and the wrong syscall number. That matters most for this op of the three, because
	// builder-built Std emits one ITSELF in `GtRuntime.emitGtPipeNameUniquifier`, where no callee-name gate
	// can see it.
	//
	// ⚠ **`getpid()` ANSWERS A 32-BIT `pid_t` AND THE UPPER HALF OF x0 IS UNSPECIFIED**, so the answer is
	// widened here rather than captured raw. `specs-shv2/process-id.md:fits-in-a-dword` asserts the id lands
	// under 2^32, which a stale high word would break — and on x64 that property is free (writing EAX zeroes
	// the upper half architecturally), which is exactly why the arm64 lane has to buy it. The Linux syscall
	// already answers a sign-extended `long`, so the widening is the identity there and stays for the lane
	// that needs it.
	function lowerOsGetPid(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.getpid, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallGetPid)
		self.emitWidenedCallResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerOsGetPid'

	// ⭐⭐ **`osEnterBackgroundPriority(result)` ⇒ `setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0, 10)` THEN
	// `getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0)`** — two GOT calls, and the second one is the answer.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT MUST BE `setpriority` AND NOT `nice`, AND A CASE ALREADY EXISTS TO CATCH THE DIFFERENCE.**
	// `nice(10)` is RELATIVE — two calls give 10 then 20 — while `setpriority` states an ABSOLUTE value, so
	// asking twice re-applies the same one. `process-background-priority.posix-value-is-stable-across-calls`
	// is written for exactly that failure and says so in its own prose.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `int` FROM THE SET IS DISCARDED AND THE ANSWER IS A FRESH READ**, which is `StdOp`'s contract
	// rather than redundancy: capturing the set's result would report the value we asked for, which a
	// lowering that called nothing could report equally well. Only RAISING a nice value is unprivileged and
	// raising is all this does, so the write cannot be silently refused and the read cannot disagree with it.
	//
	// ⚠ **`getpriority` ANSWERS `-1` BOTH AS A VALUE AND AS ITS ERROR SPELLING, AND THAT AMBIGUITY CANNOT
	// ARISE HERE.** It is distinguished through `errno` in C; this call cannot fail — `PRIO_PROCESS` is a
	// valid `which` and `who = 0` names a process that is by construction alive — and the value it reports is
	// the 10 just written. So the op needs no `errno` read and stays a two-call sequence. The result is
	// SIGN-extended because a nice value is legitimately negative (-20..19) and a zero-extension would report
	// a nice of -10 as 4294967286.
	//
	// ⚠ The route is asked ONCE and the body is its own function, `StdToX64Conversion.lowerOsGetPid`'s shape:
	// this op makes TWO route-dependent calls, so an inline `match` per call would ask the same question twice
	// and owe a second refusal message that could only echo the first.
	function lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriority(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityDarwin(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			syscall then self.lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityLinux(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriority'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME TWO CALLS AS RAW SYSCALLS, AND ONE ARITHMETIC DIFFERENCE THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL.**
	// `getpriority`'s SYSCALL cannot answer a negative nice value directly — a negative return is how a
	// syscall reports an error — so the kernel answers `20 - nice` and libc subtracts it back. This lowering
	// is the libc: without the fold, `answers-the-posix-nice-value` would still pass by the coincidence that
	// `20 - 10` is 10, and every other nice value would be silently wrong.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO `emitWidenedCallResult` HERE, AND THE DARWIN ARM NEEDS ONE.** That widening exists because
	// AAPCS64 leaves the upper half of a register holding a C `int` UNSPECIFIED; a `svc` answers a full
	// 64-bit word, so widening it would be a second opinion about a value the kernel has already settled —
	// the same reason `mmap` and `write` are not widened three functions up.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE SCOPE OBJECTION THIS ARM USED TO PANIC WITH IS ANSWERED BY INHERITANCE, AND THE ONE CASE
	// INHERITANCE CANNOT REACH IS NAMED RATHER THAN CLAIMED AWAY.** It read: *"POSIX scopes a nice value to
	// the THREAD on Linux and to the PROCESS on Darwin, so a Linux lane owes the thread-inheritance work at
	// `StdOp.osThreadCreate` as well as a call here."* Its first answer was *"there are no other threads:
	// `osThreadCreate` is a runtime abort here"*, and the worker-M `clone` falsified that. What still holds
	// is the second: `clone` inherits its creator's nice value at birth, so every M started AFTER this call
	// starts at background priority with nothing emitted for it.
	//
	// ⛔ **AN M THAT ALREADY EXISTED KEEPS THE DEFAULT NICE**, which is a real difference from the two lanes
	// that lower a whole PROCESS in one call. Closing it needs each M's tid in its P and a walk in a chunk,
	// where this is two inline `svc`s; no case reaches it, because every `processPriority` spec reads the
	// value back on the calling thread. The `processPriority` row in `targetProvidesFacility` carries the
	// same statement beside the `true` that rests on it.
	function lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityLinux(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitPriorityScopeArgs(dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: PosixBackgroundNiceValue))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Syscall(Arm64LinuxSyscallSetPriority))

		self.emitPriorityScopeArgs(dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Syscall(Arm64LinuxSyscallGetPriority))

		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), value: LinuxGetPriorityBias))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Sub(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), lhs: arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), rhs: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityLinux'

	function lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityDarwin(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitPriorityScopeArgs(dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: PosixBackgroundNiceValue))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.setpriority)))

		self.emitPriorityScopeArgs(dstRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.getpriority)))

		self.emitWidenedCallResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityDarwin'

	// The `(which, who)` pair BOTH priority calls open with — `setpriority` then adds its value in x2. One
	// emitter rather than two, because it IS the same pair: a divergence between the scope the set used and
	// the scope the read used would change the process and then report on something else.
	function emitPriorityScopeArgs(dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), value: PosixPrioProcess))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), value: PosixPriorityWhoIsSelf))
	end 'emitPriorityScopeArgs'

	// ⭐ **A 32-BIT C RESULT BECOMING THE 64-BIT VALUE THE Std TIER READS.** AAPCS64 leaves the upper half of
	// a register holding an `int` return UNSPECIFIED, so `mov vreg, x0` alone can carry a stale high word into
	// a Maxon `int`. `lsl #32` then `asr #32` is `sxtw`, spelled with the two shift ops this backend already
	// has rather than a new one — SIGNED because these callees return `int`, and a nice value really is
	// negative above normal priority.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OTHER OS LOWERINGS IN THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DO NOT USE IT.** `sysconf`, `mmap` and `write`
	// answer a `long`/`void*`/`ssize_t` — already 64 bits wide — so widening those would be a second opinion
	// about a value the ABI has already settled. This is for the `int`-returning band only.
	function emitWidenedCallResult(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		let returnReg = arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LslImm(returnReg, src: returnReg, imm: Arm64NarrowResultShift as ShiftCount))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AsrImm(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), src: returnReg, imm: Arm64NarrowResultShift as ShiftCount))
	end 'emitWidenedCallResult'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE HOST-SURFACE LOWERINGS: ARGUMENT MOVES AND A `bl` INTO A RUNTIME CHUNK.** Every one of the
	// five below is out-param shaped — the OS answer must be STORED THROUGH A POINTER the caller supplied —
	// and NEITHER POSIX lane answers through that pointer: Darwin returns nanoseconds in a register and Linux
	// fills a `timespec` of its OWN, so on both the caller's pointer has to outlive the call. An isel cannot
	// arrange that: an operand that dies at its op is coloured caller-saved, which is correct for every other
	// OS lowering in this file and fatal for these. `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s header carries the full argument;
	// the consequence here is that these lower like ORDINARY CALLS, which is also why they need no
	// `emitOsPrimitiveCall`.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `bl` TO A CHUNK IS A `CallFixup`, SO THE CHUNK MUST EXIST** — each lane's `append…RuntimeChunks`
	// emits it under the SAME `RuntimeUsage` bit that built the Std function calling into here, which is
	// what makes "the symbol is missing" unreachable rather than merely unlikely.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO `os` TEST HERE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OMISSION — IT IS WHAT THE SHARED `mrt_host_*` SYMBOLS BUY.**
	// Both POSIX lanes build a chunk under each of these names (`Arm64DarwinRuntime` /
	// `Arm64LinuxRuntime`, one or the other per image), so the `bl` is correct on either without asking.
	// A target that provides NEITHER — a `linux` program is refused only for the families its
	// `targetProvidesFacility` row still denies — is refused at its own source span (E3104) long before
	// lowering, exactly as a wasm one is. A defensive `os` branch here would be a second copy of that table
	// with no way to keep the two honest.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THAT ARGUMENT DOES NOT TRANSFER TO THE INLINE LOWERINGS ABOVE, WHICH IS WHY THEY ASK AND
	// THESE DO NOT.** The difference is the FAILURE MODE of getting it wrong, not the confidence. A `bl` into
	// a chunk a lane never built is a `CallFixup` naming a symbol that was never laid out, and
	// `arm64ResolveCallFixups` PANICS on it (`bl to unknown function '…'`) — loud, at build time, naming the
	// symbol. An `arm64ImportCall` on the Linux lane is silent: `ElfWriter` has no import-relocation consumer
	// at all, so the unpatched `adrp x16 / ldr x16 / blr x16` SHIPS, jumping through the first word of its own
	// `.text` page. So the ops that emit an import call directly must ask the route themselves; the ops that
	// branch to a chunk are already guarded by the linker step.
	function lowerOsReadWallClock(ftPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(ftPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostWallClockName))
	end 'lowerOsReadWallClock'

	// The monotonic counter and its frequency — two out-params, two argument registers, one chunk.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE ONE OF THE FIVE THAT STILL BRANCHES TO A LANE-PRIVATE SYMBOL**, because arm64-linux has
	// no body for it: `monotonicClock` is reached only through `__gt_now_ns`, a SCHEDULER entry, so it lands
	// with the green-thread floor rather than with the two clocks L3 brought over. The name changes to
	// `mrt_host_monotonic_clock` on the day a second lane builds one, exactly as the three around it did.
	function lowerOsReadClock(ticksPtrId ValueId, freqPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(ticksPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(freqPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostMonotonicClockName))
	end 'lowerOsReadClock'

	function lowerOsThreadCpuTicks(outPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(outPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostThreadCpuName))
	end 'lowerOsThreadCpuTicks'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAC2: the executable path, and THIS IS WHERE THE OP'S FIELD ORDER MEETS THE C ONE.** The op reads
	// `(result, bufLen, bufPtr)` because that is `osDirCurrentPath`'s shape and one shape is better than two
	// at the target-neutral tier; `_NSGetExecutablePath` takes the BUFFER first and the size second, exactly
	// as `GetModuleFileNameA` does, and `readlinkat` takes them third and fourth after a dirfd and a path.
	// `StdOp.osExecutablePath` states that the transposition belongs in the lowering — the only place that
	// knows an ABI — and this is that place on both arm64 lanes.
	//
	// It is a CHUNK and not an inline sequence for this band's structural reason: the caller's pointer must
	// still be live after a call that answers in a register, twice over on Darwin (the `realpath`
	// canonicalization is a second such call), and only a frame can hold it. The answer needs no widening —
	// each lane's chunk computes a byte count and a capacity, both already 64-bit.
	function lowerOsExecutablePath(resultId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufLenId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostExePathName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsExecutablePath'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAC4/5: A HOST-SURFACE CHUNK CALL — MOVE THE OPERANDS INTO x0.. IN ORDER, THEN `bl`.** Thirteen
	// filesystem ops take this shape and differ only in their arity, in whether there is an answer, and (for
	// the working directory) in the ORDER the op names its operands versus the order C does. Written once,
	// because thirteen hand-written argument sequences are thirteen chances to transpose a buffer and a
	// length — which `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s `arm64PosixMillisToTimespec` makes the same argument about.
	//
	// ⚠ The symbol it branches to is one of the SHARED `mrt_host_*` names (`Arm64PosixRuntime`), so this is
	// not a Darwin lowering wearing a neutral name: the arm64-Linux surface builds a chunk under each of them
	// too, and which body an image gets is decided once, by `buildArm64Backend`, from `target.os`.
	//
	// ⚠ The moves are emitted IN ORDER and that is what makes them sound: an operand coloured into an
	// argument register the sequence has already written is prevented by the physical def of that register,
	// exactly as `lowerCall`'s own argument moves are.
	function emitHostChunkCall(dstRef BlockRef, chunk ByteArray, args ValueIdArray)
		var index = 0 as ParamCount
		for arg in args 'eachArg'
			self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(arm64ArgRegisterAt(index)), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(arg)))
			index = index + 1
		end 'eachArg'
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(chunk))
	end 'emitHostChunkCall'

	// The same call with its answer captured. No widening: every chunk in this band computes its own answer
	// as a full 64-bit value (a BOOL, a byte count, a handle, an attribute mask), so a second opinion about
	// its upper half would be `emitWidenedCallResult`'s note in reverse.
	function lowerHostChunkValue(resultId ValueId, args ValueIdArray, chunk ByteArray, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: chunk, args: args)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerHostChunkValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CREATE IS THE ONE MEMBER OF THE NAMED-SECTION BAND WHOSE BODY DIFFERS BY LANE.** `shm_open` is
	// a libSystem call on Darwin and a libc FUNCTION on Linux — all it does there is prepend `/dev/shm/` and
	// `open` the result — so a libc-less static image builds that path itself. The mapping and the unmapping
	// are one `mmap`/`munmap` body under the shared prefix and need no route at all.
	function namedSegmentCreateChunk() returns ByteArray
		return match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall gives DarwinShmCreateName
			syscall gives LinuxShmCreateName
		end 'route'
	end 'namedSegmentCreateChunk'

	// ⭐ **THE ATTACH SPLITS ON HOW MANY OPERANDS ITS CHUNK READS, NOT ONLY ON WHICH CHUNK.** macOS's
	// `getenv` answers a POINTER `shm_open` takes directly, so the scratch buffer and its length go nowhere;
	// arm64-Linux reads the value into that buffer with `mrt_host_env_read` and then opens the path built
	// from it, which is `GetEnvironmentVariableA`'s own shape (`StdOp.osOpenNamedSegment`).
	function lowerNamedSegmentAttach(resultId ValueId, namePtrId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: argsOf(namePtrId), chunk: DarwinShmOpenName, dstRef: dstRef)
			syscall then self.lowerHostChunkValue(resultId, args: threeArgs(namePtrId, second: bufPtrId, third: bufLenId), chunk: LinuxShmOpenName, dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerNamedSegmentAttach'

	// `shm_unlink(namePtr)`. One import and no chunk on macOS — there is nothing to test and no answer to
	// fold; a chunk on arm64-Linux, because the name has to become a `/dev/shm` path first. The status is
	// discarded either way for `mrt_host_file_close`'s reason: a name that could not be removed leaves a
	// caller nothing to do.
	function lowerOsUnpublishNamedSegment(namePtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(namePtrId)))

		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.shmUnlink)))
			syscall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(LinuxShmUnlinkName))
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsUnpublishNamedSegment'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE OPEN IS INLINE ON macOS AND A CHUNK ON LINUX, AND THE SPLIT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE.**
	//
	// ⚠ **macOS: THE READ HALF IS `open` AND THE WRITE HALVES ARE `creat`, WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS THEM INLINE.**
	// `open(path, oflag, ...)` takes its permission bits as a VARARG, and AAPCS64 passes a variadic argument
	// on the STACK — which an isel has no frame to write to. `creat(path, mode)` is
	// `O_CREAT|O_TRUNC|O_WRONLY` with a NAMED mode, i.e. exactly `CreateFileA(GENERIC_WRITE, CREATE_ALWAYS)`,
	// so the one argument that differs between the three rides x1 in every case.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE macOS ANSWER IS WIDENED BECAUSE A DESCRIPTOR IS AN `int`.** AAPCS64 leaves the upper half of an
	// `int` return unspecified, and the caller compares the answer against `InvalidHandleValue` (-1) — so a
	// stale high word would make a failed open look like a live descriptor.
	//
	// ⭐ **LINUX HAS NO VARARG PROBLEM AND A DIFFERENT ONE INSTEAD.** `openat(AT_FDCWD, path, flags, mode)`
	// takes its mode in a REGISTER, so all three opens are one syscall with two immediates — but a raw `svc`
	// answers `-errno` where the Std tier tests for exactly `-1`, and there is no libc `errno` for
	// `osLastError` to read afterwards. Both of those are the chunk's work (`mrt_host_file_open`), which is
	// why this lane branches into one rather than emitting the syscall here.
	function lowerOsFileOpen(resultId ValueId, pathPtrId ValueId, importSlot OsImportSlot, darwinArgument MachineWord, linuxFlags MachineWord, linuxMode MachineWord, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(pathPtrId)))
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsFileOpenDarwin(resultId, importSlot: importSlot, argument: darwinArgument, dstRef: dstRef)
			syscall then self.lowerOsFileOpenLinux(resultId, flags: linuxFlags, mode: linuxMode, dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsFileOpen'

	// The libSystem arm: the one differing argument into x1, the import, and the descriptor widened.
	function lowerOsFileOpenDarwin(resultId ValueId, importSlot OsImportSlot, argument MachineWord, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), value: argument))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(importSlot)))
		self.emitWidenedCallResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerOsFileOpenDarwin'

	// The syscall arm: the flag word and the permission mask into x1/x2, and the chunk that turns a `-errno`
	// into the `-1`-plus-stashed-reason pair the Std tier reads. No widening — the chunk's answer is already
	// a full 64-bit descriptor or a full 64-bit -1.
	function lowerOsFileOpenLinux(resultId ValueId, flags MachineWord, mode MachineWord, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), value: flags))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: mode))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostFileOpenName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsFileOpenLinux'

	// `CloseHandle` on a file ⇒ `close(fd)` — libSystem's on macOS, `svc` on Linux, and NOTHING else differs:
	// the descriptor is in x0 either way and the `int` both answer is DISCARDED, which is the op's own
	// contract rather than this lowering's laziness (`StdOp.osFileClose` defines no result, because a failed
	// close leaves a caller nothing to do). Since nothing is read back, the Linux arm needs no chunk and no
	// errno stash — it is `lowerOsFreePages`'s shape exactly.
	function lowerOsFileClose(handleId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.close, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)
	end 'lowerOsFileClose'

	// ==========================================================================
	// The SOCKET band
	// ==========================================================================

	// ⭐⭐ **`osSocketStartup(result)` — THE ONE OP HERE THAT REACHES NO KERNEL, ANSWERED RATHER THAN
	// CALLED.** `WSAStartup(2.2, &wsadata)` binds a process to a `ws2_32` version; BSD sockets have no such
	// rendezvous — the first `socket(2)` is the whole of the initialization — so there is nothing to call and
	// nothing that can fail. The op's contract is *0 on success*, and `PosixCallSucceeded` is that 0.
	//
	// ⛔ **A REFUSAL HERE WOULD REFUSE EVERY SOCKET PROGRAM ON THIS LANE OVER A CALL WITH NO WORK TO DO** —
	// `osHandleSetInherit`'s case one band over, except that this op must still produce a VALUE, which is why
	// it emits an instruction rather than nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ The `WSADATA` operand is deliberately unread: it is a scratch buffer the Windows API demands, and the
	// Std tier frees it the moment the call returns.
	function lowerOsSocketStartup(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), value: PosixCallSucceeded as MachineWord))
	end 'lowerOsSocketStartup'

	// ⭐ **`getaddrinfo(host, NULL, hints, out)` — four register arguments and macOS's alone.** The service
	// argument is always NULL; see the op's own header for why no port is ever formatted as a string.
	function lowerOsSocketResolve(resultId ValueId, hostPtrId ValueId, hintsPtrId ValueId, outPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsSocketResolveDarwin(resultId, hostPtrId: hostPtrId, hintsPtrId: hintsPtrId, outPtrId: outPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			syscall then panic("lowerStdToArm64: `osSocketResolve` asks for `getaddrinfo`, which is a libc FUNCTION rather than a syscall — it reads `/etc/nsswitch.conf` and `/etc/resolv.conf` and speaks DNS, and a raw static image links nothing that does. This lane resolves through a DIFFERENT Std graph (`ManagedSocketRuntime.SocketResolveShape.builtinResolver`) which never emits this op, so reaching here means the addrinfo graph was installed for a target that has no `getaddrinfo`")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsSocketResolve'

	// The libSystem arm. The `int` is WIDENED for `lowerOsFileOpenDarwin`'s reason: the Std tier compares the
	// answer against 0, and a stale high word makes a successful resolve read as a failed one.
	function lowerOsSocketResolveDarwin(resultId ValueId, hostPtrId ValueId, hintsPtrId ValueId, outPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(hostPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), value: SocketNoServiceName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(hintsPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x3), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(outPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.getaddrinfo)))
		self.emitWidenedCallResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerOsSocketResolveDarwin'

	// `freeaddrinfo(list)` — one register argument, nothing captured (the call returns void). Refused on the
	// syscall route for its producer's reason: a lane with no `getaddrinfo` allocates no list to release.
	function lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfoPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfoDarwin(addrInfoPtrId, dstRef: dstRef)
			syscall then panic("lowerStdToArm64: `osSocketFreeAddrInfo` releases a list `getaddrinfo` allocated, and this lane has no `getaddrinfo` to allocate one — it resolves through `ManagedSocketRuntime.SocketResolveShape.builtinResolver`, which emits neither op. Reaching here means the addrinfo graph was installed for a target that has no `getaddrinfo`")
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfo'

	// The libSystem arm: one pointer argument and a call whose `void` return there is nothing to capture. Its
	// own function because a `match` arm carries one statement and this is two — `lowerOsCpuCountDarwin`'s
	// split, for its reason.
	function lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfoDarwin(addrInfoPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrInfoPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.freeaddrinfo)))
	end 'lowerOsSocketFreeAddrInfoDarwin'

	// `socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP)` — the three protocol arguments are the Std tier's
	// (`ManagedSocketRuntime`'s protocol block owns them, because the `hints` it fills must agree with them),
	// and all three values are portable.
	function lowerOsSocketCreate(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), value: AfInet as MachineWord))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), value: SockStream as MachineWord))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: IpprotoTcp as MachineWord))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.socket, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallSocket)
		self.emitSocketIntResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerOsSocketCreate'

	// `connect(sock, addr, sizeof(sockaddr_in))` — the length is the Std tier's `SockaddrInBytes`, which is
	// also the size of the block it allocated for the address. ⚠ MEASURED on Darwin: an `addrlen` below 16 is
	// refused with `EINVAL`, so the two really are one number.
	function lowerOsSocketConnect(resultId ValueId, sockId ValueId, addrPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sockId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: SockaddrInBytes as MachineWord))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.connect, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallConnect)
		self.emitSocketIntResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerOsSocketConnect'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO TRANSFERS — ONE EMITTER, AND THE TWO ROUTES DO NOT EVEN AGREE ON THE CALL'S ARITY.**
	// libSystem exports `send`/`recv` (four arguments); AArch64's syscall table has neither, only
	// `sendto`/`recvfrom` (six), so the Linux arm supplies the two address arguments a stream socket does not
	// use. Each caller names BOTH routes' identity, exactly as `emitOsPrimitiveCall`'s callers do, so a
	// direction cannot be wired to the right import slot and the wrong syscall number.
	function lowerOsSocketTransfer(resultId ValueId, sockId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, lenId ValueId, importSlot OsImportSlot, syscallNumber MachineWord, suppressSigpipe bool, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sockId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lenId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x3), value: self.socketTransferFlags(suppressSigpipe)))

		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(importSlot)))
			syscall then self.emitLinuxSocketTransferCall(syscallNumber, dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'route'

		self.emitSocketTransferResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerOsSocketTransfer'

	// The syscall arm's two extra arguments: `sendto`/`recvfrom` take a peer address and its length, and a
	// CONNECTED stream socket has neither to give — a NULL pair is what makes the six-argument call mean the
	// four-argument one.
	function emitLinuxSocketTransferCall(syscallNumber MachineWord, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x4), value: LinuxSocketNoPeerAddress))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x5), value: LinuxSocketNoPeerAddress))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Syscall(syscallNumber))
	end 'emitLinuxSocketTransferCall'

	// The flag word a transfer is given. ⚠ **`MSG_NOSIGNAL` IS A SEND-ONLY FLAG AND ITS VALUE IS PER-OS**
	// (`DarwinMsgNoSignal`/`LinuxMsgNoSignal` — see the first for the SIGPIPE measurement), so the receive
	// direction passes the same `SendRecvNoFlags` zero Windows passes for both directions.
	function socketTransferFlags(suppressSigpipe bool) returns MachineWord
		if not suppressSigpipe 'receiving'
			return SendRecvNoFlags as MachineWord
		end 'receiving'

		return match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall gives DarwinMsgNoSignal as MachineWord
			syscall gives LinuxMsgNoSignal
		end 'route'
	end 'socketTransferFlags'

	// ⭐⭐ **A C `int` ON THE IMPORT ROUTE, A `-errno` ON THE SYSCALL ROUTE, AND THE Std TIER READS NEITHER.**
	// It tests the answer against `InvalidSocket`/`SOCKET_ERROR` — both exactly -1 — because that is what
	// Winsock reports. libSystem reports -1 in a 32-bit `int` whose high word AAPCS64 leaves unspecified, and
	// a raw `svc` reports `-errno`, which is negative and almost never -1. Both are folded into the OP's
	// vocabulary here rather than by giving the Std tier a per-lane comparison, which is the discipline
	// `PosixRuntime`'s errno block states: one lane's vocabulary becomes the op's contract, never the reverse.
	function emitSocketIntResult(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emitWidenedCallResult(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
			syscall then self.emitSyscallFailureSentinel(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'route'
	end 'emitSocketIntResult'

	// The same fold for the two transfers, whose import-route answer is a `ssize_t` and is therefore NOT
	// widened: it is already 64 bits, so sign-extending from bit 31 would truncate any count above 2^31 —
	// `emitWidenedCallResult`'s own warning about `mmap` and `write`, one band over.
	function emitSocketTransferResult(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
			syscall then self.emitSyscallFailureSentinel(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'route'
	end 'emitSocketTransferResult'

	// ⭐ **`answer | (answer asr 63)` — BRANCHLESS, EXACT, AND IT NEEDS NO FRAME.** An arithmetic shift by the
	// sign bit's distance leaves all-ones for a negative answer and zero for a non-negative one, so the OR
	// collapses every `-errno` to -1 and leaves every descriptor and byte count untouched. That is why the
	// socket band needs no chunk on this lane where the file band does: the file surface must also PUBLISH the
	// errno for `osLastError` to classify, and `__ManagedSocketError` has no errno-classified variant to
	// publish into (`ManagedSocketRuntime.emitSocketTransferOutcome` states that).
	function emitSyscallFailureSentinel(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		let answer = arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)
		let sign = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AsrImm(sign, src: answer, imm: LinuxSyscallSignShift))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Orr(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), lhs: answer, rhs: sign))
	end 'emitSyscallFailureSentinel'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAC6: `GetStdHandle(which)` ⇒ THE DESCRIPTOR ITSELF, WITH NO CALL AT ALL.** Win32 numbers its
	// three selectors DOWNWARDS from `(DWORD)-10` while POSIX numbers its three descriptors UPWARDS from 0,
	// so the whole map is one subtraction from the input selector: `fd = StdInputHandleSelector - which`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SELECTOR IS A VALUE OPERAND AND NOT AN IMMEDIATE**, which is why this is arithmetic rather
	// than a compile-time answer: since the subprocess rung `SubprocessRuntime`'s `inherit` stdio kind picks
	// among the three at runtime.
	//
	// ⚠ The relation the subtraction rests on is ASSERTED rather than trusted — see
	// `ConsoleRuntime.assertStdHandleSelectorsMapToPosixFds`, which fails the build if the two families ever
	// stop being consecutive runs.
	function lowerOsStdHandle(resultId ValueId, whichId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		let base = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(base, value: StdInputHandleSelector as MachineWord))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Sub(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), lhs: base, rhs: TargetVReg.virtual(whichId)))
	end 'lowerOsStdHandle'

	// The one member of this band that is NOT out-param shaped — it takes a value and returns nothing — so
	// it could have been inline. It is a chunk anyway, because the millisecond→`timespec` split needs two
	// frame words to hand `nanosleep` a `struct timespec *`, and a frame is exactly what a chunk has.
	function lowerOsSleepMs(msId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(msId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostSleepMsName))
	end 'lowerOsSleepMs'

	// Lower `osFreePages(addr, size)` to POSIX `munmap(addr, size)` — the free counterpart of `lowerOsAllocPages`
	// directly above, and the shape x64's `lowerOsFreePagesLinux` takes, generalized to both OSes this backend
	// serves. Two register arguments in x0/x1 on BOTH (AAPCS64 passes a libSystem call's there, and Linux's `svc`
	// reads its arguments from the same registers), so only the terminal call differs — which is the one thing
	// `emitOsPrimitiveCall` exists to decide. The `int` it answers in x0 is NOT captured: `osFreePages` defines no
	// value, because a failed unmap leaves a caller nothing to do.
	//
	// ⭐ **THIS ARM USED TO PANIC, AND THE VOCABULARY IS WHY.** `osFreePages` carried the BASE ALONE, and there
	// is no `munmap` spelling that takes one — so the refusal here was forced by the op's shape rather than by
	// anything arm64 cannot do. F1a gave the op its length; this is the lowering that was always available.
	function lowerOsFreePages(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.munmap, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap)
	end 'lowerOsFreePages'

	// Lower `osDecommitPages(addr, size)` to `madvise(addr, size, <advice>)` — drop the range's physical backing
	// and KEEP the mapping, so the base stays valid and `osCommitPages` can re-back it in place. Same three
	// register arguments on both OSes; only the ADVICE and the terminal call differ, and each comes from its own
	// one door (`madviseDecommitAdvice`, `emitOsPrimitiveCall`).
	//
	// ⚠ The two OSes do not promise the same CONTENTS afterwards — Linux refaults zeros, Darwin's `MADV_FREE`
	// may hand the same dirty page back. `StdDialect.osDecommitPages` carries that obligation; nothing here may
	// paper over it, because there is no Darwin advice that both keeps the reservation and promises zeros.
	function lowerOsDecommitPages(addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: madviseDecommitAdvice(self.target.os)))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.madvise, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMadvise)
	end 'lowerOsDecommitPages'

	// Lower `osReservePages(size)` to `mmap(NULL, size, PROT_NONE, MAP_ANON|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)` — Go's
	// `sysReserveOS` (`vendor/go/src/runtime/mem_linux.go:163`, `mem_darwin.go:57`) verbatim, and
	// `lowerOsAllocPages` above with ONE argument changed: the protection word. `PROT_NONE` is what makes it a
	// reservation rather than an allocation — the kernel records the range and backs nothing, and a touch faults.
	//
	// The anon-private flag word still comes from `mmapAnonPrivateFlags`, so the one number that differs between
	// the two OSes this backend serves stays stated in one place.
	function lowerOsReservePages(resultId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), value: MmapKernelChoosesBase))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: MmapProtNone))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x3), value: mmapAnonPrivateFlags(self.target.os)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x4), value: MmapAnonFd))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x5), value: MmapNoOffset))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.mmap, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsReservePages'

	// Lower `osCommitPages(addr, size)` to `mmap(addr, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
	// MAP_ANON|MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_FIXED, -1, 0)` — Go's `sysMapOS` (`mem_linux.go:172`, `mem_darwin.go:67`)
	// verbatim. POSIX has no commit state: the MAPPING is the commitment, so committing a sub-range of a
	// `PROT_NONE` reservation means re-mapping it with real protection, and `MAP_FIXED` is what makes the kernel
	// use the address it was given instead of choosing one.
	//
	// ⛔ It therefore DESTROYS whatever was mapped in the range. `StdDialect.osCommitPages` carries that
	// obligation for all four lanes at once; nothing here may soften it.
	//
	// `MAP_FIXED` is ORed into the anon-private word at this one site, exactly as the x64 isel does it, so the
	// per-OS half of the flag word is still stated only in `mmapAnonPrivateFlags`.
	function lowerOsCommitPages(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, sizeId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(sizeId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), value: MmapProtReadWrite))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x3), value: mmapAnonPrivateFlags(self.target.os) or MmapFixed))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x4), value: MmapAnonFd))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x5), value: MmapNoOffset))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.mmap, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsCommitPages'

	// Lower `memFill(dest, byteCount, value)` — the destination and the length into the two registers the size
	// ladder reads, then the macro-op that IS the ladder. The exact twin of x64's `lowerMemFill`, and — unlike
	// every OS primitive around it — NOT routed through `arm64OsCallKind`: a byte fill is arithmetic and
	// stores, identical on macOS and Linux, so there is nothing for an OS to decide.
	//
	// The fill BYTE is moved nowhere: it is broadcast across the word at compile time and rides the op as an
	// immediate (`memFillPatternWord`), which is the whole reason `StdOp.memFill`'s value is a constant.
	function lowerMemFill(destId ValueId, byteCountId ValueId, value ParsedInt, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysVReg(Arm64MemFillCursorRegNum), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(destId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysVReg(Arm64MemFillCountRegNum), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(byteCountId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MemFill(memFillByte(value)))
	end 'lowerMemFill'

	// Lower `osWriteStdout`/`osWriteStderr(result, buf, len)` to `write(fd, buf, len)`. FAR simpler than
	// x64's `lowerOsWriteStream` (GetStdHandle then WriteFile with a handle, an out-param and a stack
	// argument): a single POSIX `write` with three register arguments, and the Linux syscall takes them
	// in the same x0/x1/x2 the libSystem call does. `buf`/`len` move forward into x1/x2 — a `len`
	// colouring x1 is forbidden by the physical def of x1 that reads `buf`, exactly as `lowerCall`'s arg
	// moves are sound in order.
	//
	// ⭐ ONE GRAPH FOR BOTH STREAMS, x64's reason: the descriptor is the whole difference, and it comes from
	// the shared `posixStreamFd` so the two POSIX isels cannot disagree about it. The SAME
	// `OsImportSlot.write` serves both — a descriptor is an argument, not a symbol.
	//
	// The result is `write`'s own answer in x0 (the byte count, or a negative errno), captured exactly as
	// `lowerOsAllocPages` captures `mmap`'s base.
	function lowerOsWriteStream(resultId ValueId, bufId ValueId, lenId ValueId, stream OsOutputStream, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), value: posixStreamFd(stream)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lenId)))
		self.emitOsPrimitiveCall(dstRef, importSlot: OsImportSlot.write, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallWrite)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsWriteStream'

	// Lower `atomicCas`/`atomicRmw` (F2) — ONE macro-op each, operands left VIRTUAL. Unlike every OS
	// primitive above, and unlike `lowerMemFill`'s pre-moves into fixed registers, there is nothing to
	// marshal: the LL/SC loop reads its three (or two) operands wherever the allocator put them and keeps
	// its own working state in the x16/x17 IP scratch pair, which is outside the allocatable pool. The
	// retry BRANCHES live inside the encoder's byte sequence — the Std→Target lowerings cannot create
	// blocks — exactly as `arm64JumpTable` and `arm64MemFill` already do.
	//
	// ⚠ Not routed through `arm64OsCallKind`, for `lowerMemFill`'s reason: an atomic is an ISA fact, and
	// LDAXR/STLXR are the same instructions on macOS and Linux.
	function lowerAtomicCas(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, expectedId ValueId, replacementId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AtomicCas(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), addrReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), expectedReg: TargetVReg.virtual(expectedId), replacementReg: TargetVReg.virtual(replacementId)))
	end 'lowerAtomicCas'

	function lowerAtomicRmw(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, operandId ValueId, opcode StdAtomicRmwOpcode, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64AtomicRmw(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), addrReg: TargetVReg.virtual(addrId), operandReg: TargetVReg.virtual(operandId), opcode: opcode))
	end 'lowerAtomicRmw'

	// ==========================================================================
	// MAC3 — the green-thread floor's host lowerings
	// ==========================================================================

	// ⭐⭐ **THIS BLOCK USED TO OPEN WITH ONE BLANKET REFUSAL FOR ELEVEN OPS, AND L4 DELETED IT.**
	// `requireDarwinSchedulerRoute` said the scheduler's host objects "are libSystem objects … and a raw
	// static Linux image links no libc to provide any of them". That was the whole of the gap and it is
	// closed: `Arm64LinuxRuntime` builds a `futex`-based twin of every one, under the SAME `mrt_host_*`
	// symbol, so nine of the eleven lowerings below are now unconditional — the isel branches to a name and
	// exactly one lane's body is laid out beside it (`Arm64PosixRuntime`'s header).
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO ARE NOT, AND EACH SAYS WHY AT ITS OWN SITE**, because their difference is structural rather
	// than a missing body: `osLockEnter`/`osLockLeave` are a bare import call on macOS and a chunk here, and
	// `tlsSetValue` is one `pthread_setspecific` there and a guarded store through `TPIDR_EL0` here. A third,
	// `osThreadCreate`, refuses on this lane for a reason that is not "unimplemented" at all — see its arm.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE STANDING HAZARD THE OLD DOOR EXISTED FOR IS UNCHANGED**: an unpatched import relocation SHIPS
	// SILENTLY on ELF (`ElfWriter` has no import-relocation consumer at all), so a lowering that emits
	// `arm64ImportCall` on a Linux lane jumps through the first word of its own `.text` page rather than
	// failing to link. That is why every arm below that still emits one asks the route first.

	// `tlsAlloc(result)` ⇒ `pthread_key_create(&key, NULL)`, through a chunk: the key is an OUT-PARAM and an
	// isel has no frame to give the pointer. The chunk also carries the two assertions that keep a key of 0
	// from becoming a scheduler that silently never runs.
	function lowerTlsAlloc(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostTlsAllocName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerTlsAlloc'

	// ⭐ `tlsSetValue(index, value)` — WRITE this OS thread's slot. The two arguments are marshalled once and
	// only the DESTINATION differs: `pthread_setspecific` on macOS, `mrt_linux_tls_set` on Linux.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS AN IMPORT CALL THERE AND A CHUNK HERE, WHICH IS WHY THIS OP KEEPS A ROUTE SPLIT** while its
	// eight neighbours lost theirs. `pthread_setspecific` works on any thread; the Linux store needs a slot
	// BLOCK, and a thread that never called `mrt_host_tls_alloc` has `TPIDR_EL0` = 0 — an assertion that
	// needs a branch, so it needs a body (`Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxTlsSetChunk`).
	//
	// The `int` `pthread_setspecific` answers is DISCARDED for `osEnterBackgroundPriority`'s reason — the
	// only failure it can report is an invalid key, and the key came from `mrt_host_tls_alloc`, which panics
	// rather than handing one out.
	function lowerTlsSetValue(indexId ValueId, valueId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(indexId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(valueId)))
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.pthreadSetspecific)))
			syscall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(LinuxTlsSetName))
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerTlsSetValue'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **`tlsSlotLoad(result, slotOffset)` — READ THIS OS THREAD'S SLOT WITH NO CALL AT ALL, WHICH IS
	// WHAT MAKES THE CURRENT-P READ CHEAP ENOUGH TO SIT IN A PROLOGUE GUARD.**
	//
	// Darwin's thread-specific-data array is at the THREAD POINTER: `pthread_getspecific(key)` is `tsd[key]`,
	// and MEASURED on this host with a C probe, `tsd[key] == (TPIDRRO_EL0 & ~7) + key*8`. The Std tier already
	// publishes `key*8 + tlsSlotArrayBase(target)` (0 on this OS), so the whole read is
	// **thread pointer + one indexed load** — structurally the SAME shape as the Win64 `mov dest, gs:[off]`,
	// which is why the op keeps its non-call metadata and the clobber model is unchanged.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS TSD LAYOUT IS AN EMPIRICALLY-PROBED ABI, NOT A DOCUMENTED ONE**, and the failure mode of a
	// wrong one is a WILD LOAD rather than a diagnostic. `mrt_host_tls_alloc` asserts the key it hands out
	// at run time, which is where that risk is actually paid for.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE GATE, AND WHY IT IS AN ARITHMETIC SELECT.** A slot offset of ZERO means `__sched_init_procs`
	// has not run — every reader tests the answer for null on exactly that. On Windows a zero offset cannot
	// arise (the TEB base is 0x1480); here it would read `tsd[0]`, a REAL pthread slot holding a real pointer
	// that is not a P. v1 spells the gate `CSEL dest, dest, xzr, NE`; this dialect carries no integer `csel`,
	// so the same select is a `cset` (0 or 1) and a multiply — one instruction more, no branch, and no new
	// `TargetOp` for a sequence with one caller.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE GATE CLOBBERS NZCV, AND `StdOp.tlsSlotLoad` DECLARES THAT FOR IT.** x64 lowers this op to a
	// single flag-free `mov r, gs:[off]` and needs no gate at all, so the declaration is arm64's alone —
	// the field is a union over the backends for exactly this case (see its header in StdDialect).
	// Undeclared, this sequence would sit invisibly between a fused compare and its `b.cond` and the branch
	// would read the gate's flags instead of the compare's.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO IP SCRATCHES, AND WHY x17's LIVE RANGE IS ONE OP.** `Arm64Scratch`'s header states the
	// invariant these pairs rest on: nothing a post-isel pass splices in writes x16, and a wide-frame address
	// fold — the one splice that DOES write x17 — happens at a VIRTUAL operand's use. x17 is live here only
	// across the load, whose operands are both physical, so no reload can land inside it. x16 spans the whole
	// sequence and is safe by the invariant.
	function lowerTlsSlotLoad(resultId ValueId, tebOffsetId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		let scratch = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)
		let flag = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x17)
		let offset = TargetVReg.virtual(tebOffsetId)

		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ThreadPointer(scratch, source: arm64ThreadPointerSource(self.target.os)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Add(scratch, lhs: scratch, rhs: offset))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64CmpImm(offset, imm: TlsUninitialisedSlotOffset))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Cset(flag, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(scratch, base: scratch, disp: 0, width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Mul(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), lhs: scratch, rhs: flag))
	end 'lowerTlsSlotLoad'

	// `osEventCreate(result)` ⇒ a fresh auto-reset event object. `CreateEventA(NULL, FALSE, FALSE, NULL)`
	// there, `mrt_host_wait_new(kind: event)` here.
	function lowerOsEventCreate(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64MovImm(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), value: PosixWaitKindEvent))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostWaitNewName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsEventCreate'

	// `osSetEvent(handle)` ⇒ signal it and wake one waiter.
	function lowerOsSetEvent(handleId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostEventSetName))
	end 'lowerOsSetEvent'

	// ⭐⭐ `osThreadCreate(result, entry, arg)` ⇒ `mrt_host_thread_create`, answering a waitable handle — and
	// it is ONE lowering for both POSIX lanes, because both now have a chunk under that name. The bodies are
	// as different as two host surfaces get (`pthread_create` plus a wait object there; a hand-built `clone`
	// with its own stack, guard page, slot block and join word here), and neither difference is visible from
	// this tier.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ENTRY POINT NEEDS NO THUNK ON EITHER LANE, AND THAT IS AN ABI COINCIDENCE WORTH STATING.**
	// `pthread_create`'s start routine is `void*(*)(void*)` — argument in x0, result in x0 — and shv2's own
	// internal ABI on this backend passes an integer argument in x0 and returns in x0 too, so
	// `__sched_worker_loop(p)` IS a valid start routine as compiled. The Linux chunk `blr`s it with the same
	// pair of registers for the same reason. On Windows the identical op needs the `DWORD(*)(LPVOID)` shape
	// and gets it the same way.
	//
	// ⛔ **THIS WAS A ROUTE WITH A RUNTIME ABORT ON THE LINUX ARM, AND THE ABORT DID ITS JOB.** L4 refused to
	// write a `clone` nothing could reach — `ioCompletionShape(linux)` is `inlineCompletion`, so there is no
	// completion-drain thread, and `SchedRuntime.emitSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s worker M had no producer. `spawn`
	// plus a default processor count that is the machine's gave it one, and the panic named the gap by name
	// on the first such program built for this lane rather than leaving it to a hang or a wrong answer.
	function lowerOsThreadCreate(resultId ValueId, entryPtrId ValueId, argId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(entryPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(argId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostThreadCreateName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsThreadCreate'

	// `osWaitHandle(result, handle, timeoutMs)` ⇒ the one wait that dispatches on the object's KIND — a
	// condvar wait for an event, a join for a thread. `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s layout header carries why one
	// object has to serve both.
	function lowerOsWaitHandle(resultId ValueId, handleId ValueId, timeoutMsId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(timeoutMsId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostWaitOneName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsWaitHandle'

	// `osCloseHandle(handle)` ⇒ destroy a wait object's primitives and release it.
	function lowerOsCloseHandle(handleId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(handleId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostWaitCloseName))
	end 'lowerOsCloseHandle'

	// ⭐⭐ `osEnvBlock(result)` ⇒ this process's `environ`, the POSIX shape of the question
	// `GetEnvironmentStringsA` answers on Windows. `SubprocessRuntime.environmentSnapshotShape` is what tells
	// the producer which of the two it is indexing, so nothing below the Std tier has to know.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **BOTH LANES REACH THE VECTOR THROUGH A WORD THAT HOLDS IT, AND THE DEREFERENCE IS THEREFORE
	// WRITTEN ONCE.** What differs is only how the ADDRESS OF that word is obtained. Darwin calls
	// `_NSGetEnviron`, which answers the address of `environ` rather than `environ` itself — a Mach-O main
	// executable may not name the symbol directly, which is the entire reason that accessor exists. A raw
	// static Linux image has no accessor and no libc to hold `environ` at all: its envp is the vector the
	// entry stub computed off the entry stack (`Arm64Runtime.emitArm64EnvpFromEntryStack` — it is
	// ARITHMETIC on `sp`, not a word the kernel handed over), and `__mrt_envp` is where that vector's base
	// was parked. So `arm64LeaGlobal` answers the address of the word exactly as the accessor's return value
	// does, and ONE load past either is the vector.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONE INDIRECTION, NOT TWO, AND THE SYMMETRY IS THE PROOF.** The same load also feeds `execve` in
	// `Arm64LinuxRuntime`'s spawn chunk and the walk in its `mrt_host_env_read` — both do
	// `arm64LinuxEmitDataAddr` then exactly one `ldr`, which is the reading this isel has to agree with.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ANSWER IS PARKED IN x16 ACROSS THE LOAD** for `lowerTlsSlotLoad`'s reason, stated in full at
	// `Arm64Scratch`: nothing a post-isel pass splices in writes x16, so no reload can land between the
	// dereference and the capture.
	function lowerOsEnvBlock(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		let scratch = arm64PhysReg(Arm64Scratch)

		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.lowerEnvironSlotAddrDarwin(scratch, dstRef: dstRef)
			syscall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64LeaGlobal(scratch, label: LinuxEnvpLabel))
		end 'route'

		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(scratch, base: scratch, disp: 0, width: X64AccessWidth.word64))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: scratch))
	end 'lowerOsEnvBlock'

	// The Darwin half of the route above: `_NSGetEnviron()` answers the address of `environ` in the return
	// register, which the caller's single load then dereferences. Its own function because a `match` arm is
	// ONE statement and this half is two — the Linux half is a single `arm64LeaGlobal` and stays inline.
	function lowerEnvironSlotAddrDarwin(scratch TargetVReg, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(OsImportSlot.nsGetEnviron)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(scratch, srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerEnvironSlotAddrDarwin'

	// ⭐ `osProcessSpawn` / `osProcessSpawnInherit` ⇒ ONE chunk. See the two arms' own comments for why the
	// inheritance flag that separates them on Windows has no POSIX twin to lower.
	// ⚠ **`envPtr` IS PASSED ON AND EACH LANE'S CHUNK CHOOSES BETWEEN IT AND THE PARENT's.** It arrives in the
	// shape that lane's spawn primitive takes — `SubprocessRuntime.SpawnEnvironmentShape` settles that one
	// tier up, so a POSIX chunk receives a finished `char *const envp[]` and not a block. A zero is
	// `Environment.inherit`, the default, and means the parent's own environment.
	function lowerOsProcessSpawn(commandId ValueId, cwdPtrId ValueId, creationFlagsId ValueId, startupPtrId ValueId, procInfoPtrId ValueId, envPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emitHostChunkCall(dstRef, chunk: HostSpawnName, args: sixArgs(commandId, second: cwdPtrId, third: creationFlagsId, fourth: startupPtrId, fifth: procInfoPtrId, sixth: envPtrId))
	end 'lowerOsProcessSpawn'

	// A RUNTIME abort naming an op that is statically reachable and dynamically unreachable — `osCancelIo`'s
	// shape on BOTH POSIX lanes, and `osThreadCreate`'s on the Linux one.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS NO ROUTE, AND IT USED TO ASK ONE WITH A HARD-CODED OP NAME INSIDE IT.** The old
	// `lowerDarwinRuntimeAbort` named `"osCancelIo"` inside itself, so a second caller would have been refused
	// under the FIRST one's name. A route is the caller's question; this is only the abort.
	function lowerRuntimeAbort(dstRef BlockRef, message ByteArray)
		self.lowerPanic(registerStringBlob(self.globalData, bytes: message).toByteArray(), dstRef: dstRef)
	end 'lowerRuntimeAbort'

	// `osEnvRead(result, namePtr, bufPtr, bufLen)` ⇒ `getenv` plus the copy Windows's API does for itself.
	function lowerOsEnvRead(resultId ValueId, namePtrId ValueId, bufPtrId ValueId, bufLenId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(namePtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x1), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x2), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(bufLenId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostEnvReadName))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerOsEnvRead'

	// `osLockInit(region)` ⇒ `mrt_host_lock_init`, a chunk so the "does a Darwin mutex fit `OsLockBytes`?"
	// assertion has a build-time home on the path it protects.
	function lowerOsLockInit(lockPtrId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lockPtrId)))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(HostLockInitName))
	end 'lowerOsLockInit'

	// ⭐ `osLockEnter`/`osLockLeave` — the region the Std tier allocated IS the lock (offset 0), so there is
	// no field address to compute at either site. One emitter for both, because they differ only in which
	// destination they name.
	//
	// ⚠ **macOS REACHES A LIBRARY CALL AND LINUX A CHUNK, WHICH IS WHY THIS PAIR KEEPS A ROUTE SPLIT.** There
	// the region is a `pthread_mutex_t` and the op is one `bl` through the GOT; here it is three words and
	// a `futex` protocol with a loop in it (`Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxLockEnterChunk`), and a loop
	// needs a body. Both are RECURSIVE by the owning thread, which is `InitializeCriticalSection`'s contract
	// and was measured as a hang when it was not.
	function lowerOsLockImport(lockPtrId ValueId, importSlot OsImportSlot, chunk ByteArray, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(arm64PhysReg(Arm64Register.x0), srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(lockPtrId)))
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ImportCall(osImportSlotIndex(importSlot)))
			syscall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64BranchLink(chunk))
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerOsLockImport'

	// Lower `currentThreadToken(result)` (F2) — the one member of this family with a genuine OS split on
	// arm64, so it is routed through the same `arm64OsCallKind` door `mmapAnonPrivateFlags` and
	// `madviseDecommitAdvice` already use rather than through a second `match os`.
	//
	// macOS: the masked thread pointer, `MRS TPIDRRO_EL0` + `LSR #3` + `LSL #3` — v1's owner token,
	// which needs no pthread key, no TSD dereference and no NULL-owner edge case.
	// Linux: the `gettid` syscall. ⚠ `TPIDRRO_EL0` is NOT the answer there — the read-only thread ID
	// register reads 0 unless something has written it, and shv2's Linux lane is a raw static binary with
	// no libc to do so. `StdOp.currentThreadToken` carries that argument in full.
	function lowerCurrentThreadToken(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		match arm64OsCallKind(self.target.os) 'route'
			importCall then self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64ThreadPointer(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), source: Arm64ThreadPointerSource.darwinMaskedReadOnly))
			syscall then self.lowerCurrentThreadTokenLinux(resultId, dstRef: dstRef)
		end 'route'
	end 'lowerCurrentThreadToken'

	// `gettid()` — a ZERO-argument syscall, so nothing is marshalled; only the answer is captured out of x0,
	// exactly as `lowerOsAllocPages` captures `mmap`'s base.
	//
	// ⚠ It goes straight to `arm64Syscall` rather than through `emitOsPrimitiveCall`, which routes by OS
	// between an import slot and a syscall number: this arm is ALREADY the Linux one, and there is no macOS
	// import to name — the macOS answer is the register read above, not a call.
	function lowerCurrentThreadTokenLinux(resultId ValueId, dstRef BlockRef)
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.arm64Syscall(Arm64LinuxSyscallGettid))
		self.emit(dstRef, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(TargetVReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: arm64PhysReg(Arm64IntReturnReg)))
	end 'lowerCurrentThreadTokenLinux'
end 'StdToArm64Lowering'
